  /* The loop is wider than it is tall, unlike the pencil, so it is sized off its own box rather
     than squared into the nib's. */
  .bonus-play .play-loop { width: 18px; height: 11px; }

  .bonus-now-soon {
    margin-top: 12px; font-family: var(--type); font-size: calc(11px + var(--text-size-adjust)); line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--ink-soft); max-width: 34ch;
    padding-left: 10px; border-left: 2px solid rgba(60, 40, 18, 0.2);
  }

  /* ---- the rest of the shelf ---- */
  .bonus-shelf { margin-top: 26px; border-top: 1px solid rgba(60, 40, 18, 0.16); padding-top: 6px; }
  .bonus-shelf-label {
    font-family: var(--type); font-size: calc(9px + var(--text-size-adjust)); letter-spacing: 1.6px; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-soft); margin: 8px 0 6px;
  }
  /* Six zines standing in ONE row, cover out. A row rather than a grid is the whole read:
     six portrait covers side by side on a ledge is a shelf, and the same six in two rows of
     three is a catalogue page.
     Wrapping and centred, so a seventh game lands as a centred orphan rather than alone at
     the left margin — there is no layout reason to add games in pairs. */
  /* Tops, not bottoms: the covers are all the same size, so aligning their TOPS stands them
     on one shelf line and lets the score lines hang below at whatever height they need. Bottom
     alignment instead lifts a zine whose record has wrapped to two lines, which reads as a
     shorter book rather than as a longer caption. */
  /* THIRTEEN, AND BACK TO FIVE ACROSS (2026-09-21). Twelve stood as a clean 6+6, and Who Held
     The Pen would have made that 6+6+1 — the lonely tile the two-row rule was written to avoid.
     Thirteen is prime, so no column count divides it and the job is picking the least bad
     remainder. Six leaves one on its own. Seven is the most even (7+6) and was rejected on
     size: the page gives the row 572px, so seven tiles measure 73px, inside the dead zone
     between a picture and a mark that the 62px floor is guarding. Five leaves 5+5+3, which is
     a short last row rather than an orphan, and it puts the tiles BACK to the 106px they were
     drawn and audited at when the shelf stood at ten. Five is also even again at fifteen,
     which is two games away rather than one — Nashville is already on the roster and already
     has a tile, so the next NEW zine makes fourteen, and fourteen reads 5+5+4. */
  .zine-row {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(5, minmax(0, 1fr)); justify-content: center;
    align-items: start; gap: 14px 10px; padding-bottom: 10px;
  }
  .zine-tile {
    /* FOUR ACROSS, and the count is in the grid above rather than in a width here: a tile
       fills its column and the column count is the only number that decides the shape. That
       replaced a measured `flex: 0 0 clamp()` which had to be re-derived every time the shelf
       grew, and which put the split at the mercy of whether a fifth tile happened to fit the
       row on a wide window.
       The shelf stood in ONE row up to seven games. Eight broke it: the notebook's page gives
       the row 482px on a narrow desktop and 572px on a wide one, so eight in a line came out
       at 62px a tile — below every size the covers are drawn and audited at (200 / 96 / 24),
       in the dead zone between a picture and a mark. Two rows of four put it at 113px. The
       old rule was defending against an UNEVEN wrap, "six and a lonely one", and eight is the
       first count that divides evenly, so the thing it feared cannot happen at this length.
       What it costs is that the shelf no longer clears the fold on a short window, which is
       a real loss and was taken knowingly.
       FIVE ACROSS SINCE TRACK BY TRACK MADE IT NINE (2026-09-18), which is deliberately NOT
       the 3x3 this note used to point at. The shelf is expected to reach ten, and five across
       is the column count that is even at ten and merely short by one at nine; 3x3 would have
       to be pulled apart again the moment a tenth game arrived. So the second row runs 5 and
       then 4, left-aligned, and the gap at its end is honest: it is a shelf with room on it
       rather than a layout that came out wrong. The old rule's "six and a lonely one" fear
       does not apply to a row four wide. Five across measures 88px on a narrow page and 106px
       on a wide one, both clear of the 62px floor that broke one-row-of-eight.
       SIX ACROSS SINCE NASHVILLE MADE IT ELEVEN (2026-09-21). Ten never arrived on five; the
       shelf went from nine to eleven, and eleven on five is 5+5+1 — the lonely one this note
       has feared since the row of eight. Six gives 6+5 with a single gap at the end of the
       second row, which is the same honest gap five gave at nine.
       What it costs is measured rather than guessed, because the floor here is real: a cover
       comes out at 83px on a wide desktop and 70px on a narrow one, against 102 and 88 at five
       across. That clears the 62px that broke one-row-of-eight, but only by eight pixels on a
       narrow page, and it is the closest this shelf has run to the floor. SEVEN ACROSS IS NOT
       AVAILABLE: it measures 57px narrow, under the floor, so a twelfth game does not get
       another column. Twelve is 6+6 on this count and needs no change at all; thirteen is where
       this has to be solved properly rather than widened again.
       The PHONE is three across below. Nine was a clean 3x3 and eleven is 3+3+3+2, which is not
       the lonely one — so the phone needs nothing here. Ten would have been 3+3+3+1 and was
       the case this note was holding open; the shelf skipped it. */
    width: 100%;
    display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center; gap: 7px; min-width: 0;
    padding: 8px 4px 7px; background: none; border: none; cursor: pointer; text-align: center;
    border-radius: 3px; position: relative;
  }
  .zine-tile:focus-visible { outline: 2px dotted rgba(43, 39, 34, 0.5); outline-offset: 2px; }
  .zine-tile-cover { display: block; width: 100%; position: relative; z-index: 1; }
  /* The booklet's thickness: two more sheets peeking out behind the cover, down the right
     edge and along the bottom. Without them a cover is a card, and a shelf of cards is a
     deck — which is the object this page spent its last redesign getting away from. */
  .zine-tile-cover::after {
    content: ""; position: absolute; inset: 3px -3px -3px 3px; z-index: -1; border-radius: 2px;
    background: #e7dcc4; box-shadow: 2px 2px 0 -0.5px #d8ccb0;
  }
  .zine-tile .zine-cover {
    width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; border-radius: 2px; position: relative;
    box-shadow: 0 3px 6px rgba(43, 39, 34, 0.3);
    transition: transform 0.18s ease;
  }
  .zine-tile:hover .zine-cover,
  .zine-tile:focus-visible .zine-cover { transform: translateY(-3px) rotate(-1.5deg); }
  .zine-tile-meta {
    font-family: var(--type); font-size: calc(8px + var(--text-size-adjust)); letter-spacing: 1px;
    text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-soft); opacity: 0.8; line-height: 1.35;
    position: relative; z-index: 1;
    /* Full width of the tile rather than shrink-to-fit, so an unbreakable line like
       "best 10 / 10" has the tile's whole measure to sit in instead of overrunning a box
       sized to something shorter. */
    align-self: stretch;
  }
  /* One unit of the record per line (see renderBonusPage): "best 10 / 10" over "swept 0:29".
     Kept off its own internal break too, so a unit is never split down the middle. */
  .zine-tile-line { display: block; white-space: nowrap; }
  /* The open leaf's record keeps its units whole for the same reason, but stays on one line:
     the leaf is wide enough that only a phone wraps it, and there it breaks between units. */
  .bonus-now-meta span { white-space: nowrap; }
  .zine-tile.is-soon .zine-tile-meta { opacity: 0.6; }
  /* The one lying open is on the shelf too, pulled out of the row rather than left out of it:
     eight fill the grid exactly, and leaving the current one out would mean a hole in the
     shelf or a ninth game written to fill a layout.
     TWO SIGNALS, AND NEITHER IS A COLOUR. The cover lifts, which is the real one and the
     honest one — a shelf tells you which you took out by the fact that it is out — and the
     record under it is written in PEN while every other tile's is in pencil. That contrast
     is doing more of the work than the weight beside it is: 700 at 8px in a typewriter face
     is a small change, and --ink against --ink-soft at full opacity is not.
     It replaced a highlighter swipe (era ink, masked to a marker shape). The swipe was louder
     but it was the only colour on this screen belonging to none of the eight covers, on a page
     whose whole job is showing eight pieces of artwork — and being a shape that has to WRAP
     the record, it changed size between a one-line "unplayed" and a two-line record and never
     sat right on both. A mark that adds nothing to the page was the better answer. If it ever
     reads too quiet, the next move is NOT a new mark: it is pushing the unselected records a
     shade lighter, which costs nothing. */
  .zine-tile.is-on .zine-cover { transform: translateY(-10px) rotate(-2.2deg); }
  .zine-tile.is-on .zine-tile-meta { color: var(--ink); opacity: 1; font-weight: 700; }

  /* The same cover, shrunk to sit beside the game's name on the play screen. */
  .bonus-cover-sm {
    width: 18px; height: 24px; display: inline-block; vertical-align: -5px; margin-right: 8px;
    border-radius: 1px; box-shadow: 0 1px 2px rgba(43, 39, 34, 0.35);
  }

  /* A tiny zine takes a bow, its loose pages fanning behind the cover. */
  .bonus-cover-play {
    position: relative; display: inline-flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
    width: 40px; height: 44px; padding: 0; margin: -10px 2px -10px -8px;
    vertical-align: middle; border: 0; background: none; color: var(--ink-accent);
    cursor: pointer; -webkit-tap-highlight-color: transparent;
  }
  .bonus-cover-play:focus-visible { outline: 2px dashed var(--ink-accent); outline-offset: 2px; }
  .bonus-cover-stack { position: relative; display: block; width: 18px; height: 24px; }
  .bonus-cover-play .bonus-cover-sm { position: relative; z-index: 2; margin: 0; vertical-align: top; }
  .bonus-cover-stack::before, .bonus-cover-stack::after {
    content: ""; position: absolute; inset: 1px; border: 1px solid #b9a074;
    background: #efe3cd; transform-origin: 10% 90%; opacity: 0;
  }
  .bonus-cover-stars {
    position: absolute; top: -8px; left: 25px; font: 24px var(--hand);
    opacity: 0; pointer-events: none;
  }
  .bonus-cover-play.is-dancing .bonus-cover-stack {
    animation: bonusCoverBow var(--cover-dance-time) ease-in-out both;
  }
  .bonus-cover-play.is-dancing .bonus-cover-stack::before {
    animation: bonusCoverPages var(--cover-dance-time) ease-in-out both; --page-fan: -27deg;
  }
  .bonus-cover-play.is-dancing .bonus-cover-stack::after {
    animation: bonusCoverPages var(--cover-dance-time) ease-in-out both; --page-fan: 24deg;
  }
  .bonus-cover-play.is-dancing .bonus-cover-stars {
    animation: bonusCoverSpark var(--cover-dance-time) ease-out both;
    text-shadow: -36px 15px 0 var(--ink-accent), 5px 38px 0 var(--ink-accent);
  }
  .bonus-cover-play.is-winking .bonus-cover-stars {
    animation: bonusCoverWink var(--cover-dance-time) ease both;
  }
  @keyframes bonusCoverBow {
    0%, 100% { transform: none; }
    18% { transform: translateY(2px) rotate(-12deg) scale(0.92); }
    43% { transform: translateY(-12px) rotate(14deg) scale(1.65); }
    65% { transform: translateY(-9px) rotate(-9deg) scale(1.5); }
    83% { transform: translateY(1px) rotate(4deg) scale(1.05); }
  }
  @keyframes bonusCoverPages {
    0%, 18%, 100% { opacity: 0; transform: rotate(0); }
    40%, 65% { opacity: 1; transform: rotate(var(--page-fan)); }
  }
  @keyframes bonusCoverSpark {
    0%, 22%, 100% { opacity: 0; transform: scale(0.6); }
    45%, 62% { opacity: 1; transform: scale(1); }
    85% { opacity: 0; transform: scale(1.15); }
  }
  @keyframes bonusCoverWink { 0%, 100% { opacity: 0; } 40%, 65% { opacity: 1; } }

  [data-reduce-motion="on"] .zine-open.drop { animation: none; }
  [data-reduce-motion="on"] .zine-tile .zine-cover { transition: none; }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .zine-open.drop { animation: none; }
  }
  /* Narrow: the zine stops being a spread and becomes a cover with its page under it. It
     has to be an explicit column — leaving it as a wrapping row lets the text leaf shrink
     to a one-word ribbon beside the cover instead of dropping below it. */
  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .bonus-head { flex-direction: column; gap: 4px; }
    .zine-open { flex-direction: column; align-items: center; max-width: 300px; }
    .zine-leaf-art {
      flex: none; width: 100%; border-right: 1px solid rgba(60, 40, 18, 0.16);
      border-radius: 3px 3px 0 0; border-bottom: none;
      box-shadow: inset 0 -14px 14px -14px rgba(43, 39, 34, 0.4);
    }
    :root[data-theme="dark"] .zine-leaf-art { box-shadow: inset 0 -14px 14px -14px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.55); }
    :root[data-theme="dark"] .zine-leaf-page { box-shadow: inset 0 14px 14px -14px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.55); }
    .zine-leaf-page {
      width: 100%; border-left: 1px solid rgba(60, 40, 18, 0.16); border-top: none;
      border-radius: 0 0 3px 3px; text-align: center;
      box-shadow: inset 0 14px 14px -14px rgba(43, 39, 34, 0.4);
    }
    .zine-leaf-page .bonus-now-blurb, .zine-leaf-page .bonus-now-soon { margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; }
    .zine-leaf-page .bonus-now-soon { text-align: left; }
    /* the staples turn with the fold: across the top of the page leaf, not down a side */
    .zine-spine { left: 0; right: 0; top: 40%; bottom: auto; width: auto; height: 0; }
    .zine-spine i { left: auto; top: -6px; width: 3px; height: 12px; }
    .zine-spine i:nth-child(1) { left: 18%; }
    .zine-spine i:nth-child(2) { left: 50%; }
    .zine-spine i:nth-child(3) { left: 81%; }
    /* Three across on a phone: a 375px page divided six ways gives a cover 55px, which is
       below the size its collage still reads at. */
    .zine-row { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
    /* THIRTEEN LEAVES A LONE TILE ON A PHONE AND NO COLUMN COUNT FIXES IT: thirteen is prime,
       and the only width that divides it evenly is five, which gives a 55px cover here. So the
       orphan is centred instead of removed — a single tile in the middle column reads as the
       end of a shelf, where the same tile hard against the left margin reads as a row that
       failed to fill. It only fires on a last tile that is really alone (`3n + 1`), so the
       clean counts are untouched and this quietly stops applying at fourteen. */
    .zine-tile:last-child:nth-child(3n + 1) { grid-column: 2; }
  }



  /* ---------- TRACK BY TRACK: the album board and the sheet ----------
     The board is TWELVE LANDSCAPE SLEEVES, pasted up out of the bonus shelf's own torn stock
     (js/sleeves.js). It is laid on its side on purpose: the shelf one click back is a rack of
     upright covers and Album Focus is a board of twelve squares, so an upright card here would
     read as one of those dealt twice. Everything inside a card is drawn in the SVG — ground,
     collage, both labels, grain — and the only thing this stylesheet owns is how many fit
     across, which is the one decision that has to change with the width of the paper. */
  /* The header wears the GAME'S OWN COVER, not a page mark. Every other panel in the notebook
     is a section of it and gets a drawn mark; this one is a game, and the little torn-paper zine
     is what means Track by Track on the shelf you took it off and again beside the title while
     you are playing it. A third object for the screen in between them would be the only place
     the game is something else. It is the play screen's markup exactly, down to the class, so
     the cover dances here too — that listener is delegated on the document and asks for nothing
     but the button. */
  .tbt-mark { display: inline-flex; align-self: center; }
  /* The play screen's button is 40px wide around an 18px cover, which is room for the encore to
     scale and lean about in a header that has nothing else on that side. Here it left 23px of
     paper between the zine and its own title. The box is pulled in to the cover and the spacing
     handed back to .title-group's gap, which is what sets it for every other panel; the dance is
     a transform, so it still throws itself around outside the box without moving anything. */
  .tbt-mark .bonus-cover-play { width: 18px; margin: -10px 0; }

  .tbt-blurb {
    font-family: var(--hand); font-size: calc(19px + var(--text-size-adjust));
    line-height: 1.4; color: var(--ink); margin: 2px 0 6px; max-width: 46ch;
  }
  .tbt-sub {
    font-family: var(--type); font-size: calc(10.5px + var(--text-size-adjust));
    letter-spacing: 1.2px; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-soft);
    margin: 0 0 12px;
  }
  /* THREE ACROSS ON THE NOTEBOOK, TWO ON A PHONE, and those are measured numbers rather than
     taste: the leaf hands this board 572px of paper on a desktop and 266px on a phone, which
     makes a card 182px and 127px wide. Four across would drop the notebook card to 134px and
     the lettering on the longest record with it, which is the size the hand stops being the
     notebook's hand. */
  .tbt-shelf {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 14px 12px; margin: 0 0 16px;
  }
  /* Left aligned, both the name lettered on the sleeve and the count under it. Everything on
     this page hangs off the same left edge — the blurb, the sub-line, the first card — and a
     centred caption under a flush-left name reads as two decisions rather than one. */
  .tbt-rec {
    display: block; width: 100%; padding: 0; border: none; background: none;
    cursor: pointer; text-align: left; border-radius: 3px;
    transition: transform 0.16s ease;
  }
  .tbt-art { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 2px; }
  /* ONE LINE, ALWAYS. The caption is the track count and nothing else, at every width the board
     is ever handed: a second line under one card and not another pulls that row out of line with
     the rest of the board, which on a grid of twelve is the first thing the eye finds. */
  .tbt-cap {
    display: block; margin-top: 5px; white-space: nowrap; padding-left: 2px;
    font-family: var(--type); font-size: calc(9px + var(--text-size-adjust));
    letter-spacing: 1px; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .tbt-rec:hover { transform: translateY(-3px) rotate(-0.6deg); }
  .tbt-rec:focus-visible { outline: 2px dotted rgba(43, 39, 34, 0.5); outline-offset: 3px; }
  .tbt-foot {
    font-family: var(--type); font-size: calc(10.5px + var(--text-size-adjust));
    color: var(--ink-soft); line-height: 1.6; margin: 0; max-width: 56ch;
  }
  /* Two across on a phone. This sits HERE rather than up in the shelf's own phone block, where
     it was first written and quietly did nothing: that block is above this section in the file,
     so the three-across rule below it won on source order alone. */
  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .tbt-shelf { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 11px 10px; }
  }

  /* ---------- The sheet itself ----------
     THE TRACKLIST IS WRITTEN ON THE PAGE'S OWN RULING, and every number below follows from
     that one decision. .card already paints ruled paper at a `--line` pitch and a red margin
     rule at `--rule-x`; the first build of this sheet ignored both and drew its own — rows on
     a 35.2px rhythm with their own border-bottom, over paper ruled at 32px, plus a second red
     margin a few pixels right of the real one. Two rulings beating against each other drift a
     whole line apart by track ten, and the numbers sat ON the card's red rule rather than in
     the margin it opens up. The page read as a table dropped onto notebook paper, which is
     exactly what it was. Now: the row IS a ruled line of the paper, and the margin the paper
     already has is where the track numbers go. */
  .tbt-sheet {
    /* Snapped onto the paper's ruling by snapTrackGrid(), which measures where the sheet
       actually lands and pushes it down by the remainder. It is a measured number and not a
       constant because everything above it — the HUD, the title, a wrapped album name — moves
       with the viewport and the text-size setting. Shifting the WHOLE sheet rather than just
       the list keeps the gap under the album name fixed; the slack is spent on the margin
       above the heading, where up to a line of it is invisible.
       PADDING and not margin: #bonusPlayBody has neither padding nor a border, so a top margin
       here collapses straight through it and moves nothing at all — which is exactly what the
       first version of this did, silently. */
    margin: 0 0 14px; padding-top: var(--tbt-snap, 0px);
    /* EVERY MARK THE SHEET MAKES IN THE RECORD'S COLOUR USES THIS, and it is the era colour
       taken two thirds of the way toward the page's own ink rather than the era colour itself.
       These are PEN marks — the line under the album's name, the nib in the margin, the line
       the pen is resting on — and a pen in folklore's grey is still a pen, where folklore's
       grey laid straight onto cream is a 1.8:1 smudge and the Tortured Poets' sand is worse.
       Mixed and at full weight the palest of the twelve comes back at 3.1:1 and the record
       still reads as itself. Measured across all twelve; do not swap it back for raw --era. */
    --tbt-ink: color-mix(in srgb, var(--era, var(--ink)) 66%, var(--ink));
  }
  .tbt-sheet-head {
    font-family: var(--hand); font-size: calc(33px + var(--text-size-adjust));
    color: var(--ink); margin: 0; font-weight: 400; line-height: 1.1;
  }
  /* The album keeps its own casing, so the kicker is what says which of the twelve games this
     is — set in the typewriter face, the notebook's voice for a printed label, and lowercase
     because it is a note to yourself rather than a masthead. */
  .tbt-sheet-kicker {
    font-family: var(--type); font-size: calc(9px + var(--text-size-adjust));
    letter-spacing: 1.8px; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-soft);
    margin: 0 0 3px;
  }
  /* A pen line under the heading in the record's own ink. It stops well short of the measure
     and fades out at the right, the way a line drawn by hand under a title does — a rule that
     ran the full width would read as a border. */
  .tbt-sheet-rule {
    /* Pulled up under the name. At its first offset it landed five pixels off one of the
       paper's own printed rules and read as a smudge on that line rather than as an underline
       of its own. */
    height: 2px; margin: -1px 0 0; max-width: 168px; border-radius: 2px;
    background: linear-gradient(90deg,
      var(--tbt-ink, var(--ink-soft)) 0%, var(--tbt-ink, var(--ink-soft)) 62%, transparent 100%);
    opacity: 0.75;
  }
  .tbt-list { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; }
  /* EVERY ROW IS EXACTLY ONE RULED LINE OF THE PAPER, written or not. The height is `--line`
     and so is the line-height, which does three jobs at once: a blank, a line of handwriting
     and a printed alt take all resolve to the same box without three line boxes being talked
     into agreeing; the sheet cannot grow as it is filled and creep down the page under the pen;
     and a title too long for the measure wraps to exactly TWO ruled lines instead of knocking
     everything below it off the grid. There is deliberately no border-bottom — the paper's own
     rule is the line, which is the whole point. */
  .tbt-row {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: 22px 1fr auto; align-items: center;
    gap: 0 18px; padding: 0;
    /* -40px puts the number column at 18–40px from the padding box, clear of the card's red
       rule at 44px. The same tie to .card's 58px left padding the achievement plate uses;
       move that padding and this follows. */
    margin-left: -40px;
    font-size: calc(20px + var(--text-size-adjust));
    line-height: var(--line); min-height: var(--line);
  }
  .tbt-n {
    position: relative; top: var(--tbt-sit);
    font-family: var(--type); font-size: calc(10.5px + var(--text-size-adjust));
    color: var(--ink-soft); text-align: right; line-height: var(--line);
  }
  /* WRITING SITS ON THE LINE, not between the lines. A 20px face centred in a 32px band puts
     its baseline about eight pixels clear of the rule, which reads as floating — the giveaway
     that the text and the paper are two separate things. `--tbt-sit` drops the ink onto the
     rule; it is a nudge in `top` rather than a transform so it cannot fight the ink-settling
     animation, and it moves nothing in the layout, so a title that wraps still takes exactly
     two ruled lines. The number and the highlighter take the same drop, or the three come
     apart from each other. */
  .tbt-row { --tbt-sit: 7px; }
  .tbt-title {
    position: relative; top: var(--tbt-sit);
    font-family: var(--hand); font-size: inherit; color: var(--ink);
  }
  /* Ink settling rather than a title appearing: a written track fades up over a beat and
     rises a pixel or two, which is as much as a pen does. It is the only motion on the sheet
     and it only ever plays on the row just written, because renderTrackSheet redraws the whole
     list and an animation on every .is-done would replay the entire record on every answer. */
  .tbt-row.is-fresh .tbt-title { animation: tbtInk 0.34s ease-out both; }
  @keyframes tbtInk {
    from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(2px); }
    to   { opacity: 1; transform: none; }
  }
  /* Nothing here opts out globally, so both of the sheet's animations say so themselves. A
     track still has to arrive written and the line still has to be drawn — only the arriving
     and the drawing go. */
  [data-reduce-motion="on"] .tbt-row.is-fresh .tbt-title { animation: none; }
  /* A blank track is a blank ruled line and NOTHING else, which is both the honest thing and
     the safe one: a dash sized off the title it hides would quietly say how long the answer is,
     and a dash of any fixed length on a full-width ruled line was the stub that made the right
     two thirds of the page read as dead. The paper is already drawing the line. */

  /* ---------- Where the pen is ----------
     Not a selected table row. The first build shaded the whole row grey and drew a hard solid
     bar in the era colour across the writing area, which read as a row hover state under a
     loading bar — the two most generic things a web page can do, on the screen that is
     supposed to be the most hand-made. What marks the place now is what would mark it on
     paper: the nib resting in the margin, the number gone over in ink, and a swipe of
     highlighter in the record's own colour over the space the answer goes. */
  .tbt-row.is-now .tbt-n { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 700; }
  /* The nib: a small ink wedge in the margin, pointing at the line being written. It sits in
     the gap between the number column and the red rule, which is the one piece of the margin
     nothing else is using. */
  .tbt-row.is-now .tbt-n::after {
    content: ""; position: absolute; left: 100%; top: 50%;
    transform: translateY(-50%); margin-left: 4px;
    width: 0; height: 0;
    border-left: 6px solid var(--tbt-ink, var(--ink));
    border-top: 4px solid transparent; border-bottom: 4px solid transparent;
    opacity: 0.85;
  }
  /* Drawn at its own size and NOT stretched to the column: a pen line scaled to fit would
     thin or fatten with the window, and the wobble in the path would stretch with it. It sits
     a shade higher than the writing does, because a line goes under the words. */
  .tbt-cur {
    position: relative; top: calc(var(--tbt-sit) + 1px);
    display: block; max-width: 100%; height: 10px;
    fill: var(--tbt-ink, var(--ink)); opacity: 0.85;
  }
  /* The pen resting on the line rather than a bar switching on. It only ever plays on the row
     the pen just moved to, which is the one row the player is looking at. */
  .tbt-row.is-now .tbt-cur { animation: tbtPen 0.4s ease-out both; transform-origin: left center; }
  @keyframes tbtPen {
    from { clip-path: inset(0 100% 0 0); }
    to   { clip-path: inset(0 0 0 0); }
  }
  [data-reduce-motion="on"] .tbt-row.is-now .tbt-cur { animation: none; clip-path: none; }
  /* An alt take: printed onto the sheet rather than asked for, because its honest answer is a
     title sitting higher up the same record. Set in the typewriter face, which is the notebook's
     way of saying a thing was printed rather than written. */
  .tbt-row.is-printed .tbt-title {
    font-family: var(--type); font-size: calc(12.5px + var(--text-size-adjust));
    color: var(--ink-soft); line-height: var(--line); top: calc(var(--tbt-sit) - 3px);
  }
  .tbt-printed {
    font-family: var(--type); font-size: calc(9px + var(--text-size-adjust));
    letter-spacing: 1px; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-soft); opacity: 0.7;
  }
  /* The one blank that can reach the finished sheet. A quit banks nothing, so in practice it
     never does — drawn in the editor's red pen rather than assumed away. */
  .tbt-rule--miss {
    display: block; height: 1.5px; width: 100%; max-width: 168px;
    background: var(--red-pen, #b0402f); opacity: 0.5;
  }

  /* THE WRITING LINE STICKS TO THE FOOT OF THE PAGE for the length of a run. A thirty-track
     record is taller than the window, so an ordinary line under the sheet ends up somewhere
     below the fold while the pen is up at track nine, and the player has to scroll between
     reading where they are and writing what comes next. Sticky keeps the two together: the
     sheet scrolls under the line the way paper moves under a hand. */
  /* The gutter is covered too. A row hangs its track number out to the left on a negative
     margin, so a sticky block that only spans the content box leaves that column showing
     through — the numbers of the rows underneath read straight over the hint line. */
  /* It spans the whole sheet of paper, both edges: -40px reaches back over the margin the
     track numbers hang in and -30px cancels .card's right padding, so the band runs clean to
     each edge of the page. Stopping at the content box left the paper's own ruling showing in
     two strips down the sides of it, which on the dark page reads as a panel floating on the
     sheet rather than as the foot of it. Both numbers are tied to .card's padding (58px left,
     30px right); move that and these follow. */
  /* IT IS PAINTED AS THE FOOT OF THE SHEET, NOT AS A PANEL ON IT. Flat --paper is not what
     the paper looks like anywhere on this page: .card lays the lamp over it, which falls into
     shade at the bottom of the sheet, exactly where this band sits. A band of raw --paper
     down there therefore comes out LIGHTER than the paper either side of it, which is the
     whole reason it read as a white slab pasted over the notebook. So --tbt-foot is --paper
     with the foot of the lamp already mixed in (the light theme's 0.075 of warm brown, the
     dark one's 0.17 of black). No era wash: .card carries none, so neither does its foot. The glow above it takes the same colour for
     the same reason — a paper-white haze rising off the band bleached the ruled lines it was
     only meant to fade out. */
  :root { --tbt-foot: color-mix(in srgb, #4a351d 7.5%, var(--paper)); }
  :root[data-theme="dark"] { --tbt-foot: color-mix(in srgb, #000000 17%, var(--paper)); }
  .tbt-pen {
    position: sticky; bottom: 0; z-index: 3; padding: 10px 30px 4px 40px;
    margin-left: -40px; margin-right: -30px;
    background: var(--tbt-foot);
    box-shadow: 0 -14px 18px -8px var(--tbt-foot);
  }
  /* The clock IS the score in this game, and the shelf's shared readout sets it at 12px under
     a bar sized for a countdown. Scoped up here only — every other game on the shelf is scored
     in points and its clock is a thing running out, not the number being played for. */
  [data-bonus-game="track-by-track"] .bg-timer-label {
    font-family: var(--hand); font-size: calc(21px + var(--text-size-adjust));
    color: var(--ink); margin-top: 5px; letter-spacing: 0.5px;
  }

  /* ---------- The finished sheet ---------- */
  .tbt-end {
    border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 4px; padding: 14px 16px 12px;
    margin: 0 0 16px; text-align: center; position: relative;
    background: rgba(43, 39, 34, 0.035);
  }
  /* 700, like every other number this notebook writes big (the personal best, the keepsake
     counter, a challenge's name). Caveat ships 400-700 in one variable file, and this was the
     only headline in the place left at 400 — which does not read as a lighter choice, it reads
     as the one bit of handwriting that did not get pressed down. */
  .tbt-end-time {
    font-family: var(--hand); font-weight: 700; font-size: calc(52px + var(--text-size-adjust));
    line-height: 1; color: var(--ink);
  }
  .tbt-end-remark {
    font-family: var(--hand); font-size: calc(20px + var(--text-size-adjust));
    color: var(--ink); margin: 6px 0 4px;
  }
  .tbt-end-meta {
    font-family: var(--type); font-size: calc(10.5px + var(--text-size-adjust));
    letter-spacing: 0.8px; color: var(--ink-soft); margin: 0 0 12px;
  }
  /* The shared row, sized for the panel it sits in rather than for the back cover: no top
     margin (the charm row above already leaves its own gap), and held in from the panel's
     edges so the pair reads as two buttons under the time and not as the foot of the box.
     It also drops the stacked row's 104px of bottom padding, which exists on the back cover
     to clear the corner links at the foot of the WINDOW — this panel is at the top of the
     page with a whole tracklist under it. */
  .tbt-end .bg-end-actions { max-width: 420px; margin: 0 auto; }
  /* The rubber stamp the back cover uses, in this game's own words. Only ever printed on a run
     that actually took the record, so it can be as loud as a stamp is. */
  .tbt-stamp {
    position: absolute; top: 8px; right: 10px; transform: rotate(-7deg);
    font-family: var(--type); font-size: calc(11px + var(--text-size-adjust));
    letter-spacing: 1.4px; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: #b0402f; border: 2px solid #b0402f; border-radius: 3px;
    padding: 2px 7px; opacity: 0.8;
  }

  /* ---------- Bonus game play screen (shared chrome for every bonus game) ---------- */
  /* Spot the Slip's short prompt should still open as a full notebook page. This is a
     minimum only: its answer card and narrow layouts remain free to extend the paper. */
  :is(#screen-bonusplay, [data-flip-id="screen-bonusplay"])[data-bonus-game="spot-the-slip"] { min-height: 540px; }
  .bg-hud {
    display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: baseline;
    font-family: var(--type); font-size: calc(10.5px + var(--text-size-adjust)); letter-spacing: 1.2px; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-soft); margin: 2px 0 10px;
  }
  /* Trimmed from 26px because the readout underneath now carries part of that gap. */
  .bg-timer { max-width: 320px; margin: 0 auto 14px; }
  .bg-timer-track { height: 8px; background: rgba(43,39,34,0.08); border-radius: 6px; overflow: hidden; }
  .bg-timer-fill {
    height: 100%; width: 100%; border-radius: 6px; background: var(--bead);
    transition: background 0.3s;
  }
  .bg-timer-fill.low { background: var(--margin); }
  /* Same readout the round screen keeps under its clock, so a bonus page tells you how long
     is left in words as well as in bar length. */
  .bg-timer-label {
    text-align: center; margin-top: 7px;
    font-family: var(--type); font-size: calc(12px + var(--text-size-adjust)); color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  /* The instruction over the page, set like the round screen's "write a song with". */
  .bg-ask {
    font-family: var(--type); font-size: calc(11.5px + var(--text-size-adjust)); letter-spacing: 1.6px; text-transform: lowercase;
    color: var(--ink-soft); text-align: center; margin: 0 0 4px;
  }
  /* The hint under the writing line, same voice as the round screen's. */
  .bg-hint {
    font-family: var(--type); font-size: calc(11px + var(--text-size-adjust)); color: var(--ink-soft);
    text-align: center; margin: 9px 0 0; min-height: 1em;
  }
  /* The writing line itself is the round screen's (.input-area + .song-input); all this does
     is hold it to a sensible measure and give the suggestion cards somewhere to hang. */
  .bg-write { max-width: 380px; margin: 0 auto; }
  /* Room under the line for the suggestion cards to land ON the page. They still overhang if
     the list runs long, which is fine — a stack of cards spilling past the edge of the paper
     is the look — but the first one should never be floating out on the desk alone. */
  .bg-write--sug { margin-bottom: 76px; }
  .bg-write .bg-reject {
    position: absolute; left: 0; right: 0; top: 100%; margin-top: 5px;
  }

  /* A song headed up like a sheet filed in the notebook: name in pen, a ruled line under it,
     the album and section noted small underneath. */
  .bg-sheet { text-align: center; margin: 2px auto 16px; max-width: 34ch; }
  .bg-sheet-title {
    font-family: var(--hand); font-weight: 700; font-size: calc(25px + var(--text-size-adjust)); line-height: 1.1;
    color: var(--ink); margin: 0;
  }
  .bg-sheet-rule {
    height: 1.5px; margin: 5px auto 6px; max-width: 15ch;
    background: linear-gradient(90deg, transparent, rgba(178,58,63,0.5) 18%, rgba(178,58,63,0.5) 82%, transparent);
  }
  .bg-sheet-meta {
    font-family: var(--type); font-size: calc(9px + var(--text-size-adjust)); letter-spacing: 1.6px; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .bg-section {
    font-family: var(--type); font-size: calc(9px + var(--text-size-adjust)); letter-spacing: 1.6px; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: #4a8c87; text-align: center; margin-bottom: 14px;
  }

  /* Spot the Slip — the doctored line, each word its own tappable chip. */
  .bg-line {
    display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; justify-content: center; align-items: baseline;
    gap: 2px 5px; max-width: 44ch; margin: 0 auto 6px;
  }
  .bg-word, .bg-word-plain {
    font-family: var(--hand); font-size: calc(27px + var(--text-size-adjust)); line-height: 1.25; color: var(--ink);
  }
  .bg-word {
    cursor: pointer; background: none; border: none; border-radius: 3px;
    padding: 1px 4px; transition: background 0.12s, transform 0.12s;
  }
  .bg-word:hover:not(:disabled) { background: rgba(199,149,31,0.22); }
  .bg-word:active:not(:disabled) { transform: translateY(1px); }
  .bg-word:disabled { cursor: default; }
  /* Reveal: the impostor is circled in red pen; a wrong tap is struck through. */
  .bg-word.is-answer {
    background: rgba(178,58,63,0.14); box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1.5px rgba(178,58,63,0.55);
    color: #8a2626;
  }
  .bg-word.is-wrong { text-decoration: line-through; text-decoration-color: rgba(43,39,34,0.5); opacity: 0.6; }

  /* Sing It Back — one word left out of the line. The gap is gone over with the chisel
     highlighter a revealed lyric wears (the stroke is the shared rule, named on .lyric-card's
     mark), so the eye lands where the game is asking and the answer, once it is written in,
     is already marked the way every other answer in the notebook is. Its width is FIXED
     whatever the answer is: sizing it to the word would hand over the letter count. It grows
     only once there is something written in it. The ruled underline is the blank itself and
     goes once the word is in. */
  .bg-blank-line { margin-bottom: 22px; }
  .bg-blank {
    display: inline-block; min-width: 4.6ch; min-height: 1.05em; text-align: center; line-height: 1.05;
    border-bottom: 1.5px solid rgba(43,39,34,0.55);
  }
  /* Reveal: the word you got is written in as if it always belonged; the one you missed goes
     in red, the same pen that circles Spot the Slip's impostor, over the wrong card's blush.
     The fixed width has done its job by now, so the stroke closes in to hug the word the way
     it hugs one on a lyric card. */
  .bg-blank:is(.is-got, .is-answer) { min-width: 0; border-bottom-color: transparent; }
  .bg-blank.is-answer { --hl: rgba(178,58,58,0.15); color: #8a2626; }

  /* Then What — a verse growing down the page, and three lines under it of which one comes
     next. The sheet reads as writing on paper; the cards read as loose lines waiting to be
     chosen, which is why only the cards get an edge. */
  .bg-chain { max-width: 40ch; margin: 0 auto 14px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 2px; }
  .bg-chain-row { position: relative; padding: 1px 8px; }
  .bg-chain-text { font-family: var(--hand); font-size: calc(20px + var(--text-size-adjust)); line-height: 1.28; color: var(--ink); }
  /* A wrong pick, in the margin above the line that corrected it: pencil rather than pen,
     smaller, struck through, with the red cross out in the rule's gutter. It is an annotation
     on the page, never part of the verse. */
  .bg-chain-row.is-miss { padding-top: 0; padding-bottom: 0; }
  .bg-chain-row.is-miss .bg-chain-text {
    font-size: calc(15px + var(--text-size-adjust)); color: rgba(43,39,34,0.42); line-height: 1.2;
  }
  .bg-chain-row.is-miss s { text-decoration-color: rgba(43,39,34,0.4); }
  .bg-chain-row.is-miss .bg-chain-text.is-late { font-style: italic; }
  /* Lines the clock never reached: the verse finishes itself, faintly, so the page still reads
     as the song without passing them off as picks. */
  .bg-chain-row.is-unsung .bg-chain-text { opacity: 0.45; }
  .bg-chain-mark { position: absolute; left: -19px; top: 50%; transform: translateY(-50%); width: 13px; }
  /* The section noted in the margin, and only where it changes — a chain that crosses a
     boundary shows where it crossed. */
  .bg-chain-sec {
    display: block; font-family: var(--type); font-size: calc(8.5px + var(--text-size-adjust)); letter-spacing: 1.5px;
    text-transform: uppercase; color: #4a8c87; margin: 5px 0 1px;
  }
  /* The newest line inks in. Nothing waits on it: the cards are dealt on their own beat. */
  .bg-chain-row.is-new .bg-chain-text {
    display: inline-block; animation: chainInk 0.34s ease-out both;
  }
  @keyframes chainInk {
    from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(-5px); }
    to { opacity: 1; transform: none; }
  }
  /* The three candidates, stacked FULL WIDTH rather than side by side: a lyric line wants to
     be read left to right, and thirds of the page make all three unreadable. The gap is
     generous on purpose — one tap commits with no undo, so the misclick has to be designed
     out rather than confirmed away. */
  .bg-chain-cards {
    max-width: 40ch; margin: 0 auto 4px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 8px;
  }
  .bg-chain-card {
    display: block; width: 100%; text-align: left; cursor: pointer;
    background: var(--card-wash, rgba(255,253,247,0.5)); border: 1px solid var(--card-edge, rgba(43,39,34,0.16));
    border-radius: 4px; padding: 8px 11px; color: var(--ink);
    transition: background 0.14s, box-shadow 0.14s, transform 0.1s;
  }
  .bg-chain-card:hover:not(:disabled) { background: rgba(199,149,31,0.14); }
  /* A white wash lifts a card off cream paper and IS the light on a dark one. Inverted, so the
     three lines still read as cards you can tap rather than as lit bars. The gold hover wash
     works on either ground and is left alone. */
  :root[data-theme="dark"] .bg-chain-card {
    --card-wash: rgba(236,229,214,0.06); --card-edge: rgba(236,229,214,0.16);
  }
  .bg-chain-card:active:not(:disabled) { transform: translateY(1px); }
  .bg-chain-card:disabled { cursor: default; }
  /* The verdict lands on the cards, where the eye already is: the real line ringed in gold,
     the wrong tap struck out in the same pen that strikes Spot the Slip's. */
  .bg-chain-card.is-answer {
    background: rgba(199,149,31,0.16); box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1.5px rgba(199,149,31,0.8);
  }
  .bg-chain-card.is-wrong { opacity: 0.6; }
  .bg-chain-card.is-wrong .bg-chain-text {
    text-decoration: line-through; text-decoration-color: rgba(178,58,63,0.6);
  }

  /* Only Here — a song's name and six of its own words dealt face up, with nothing pencilled
     in between them. (A price list lived there while the game was a typed wager; the hand
     retired the bet, so the ladder is now taught on the cards at the reveal instead.)
     The hand: six words as scraps cut out of the song's own lyric sheet. Ransom-note energy
     fits the notebook, and more practically it makes them read as FOUND words rather than as UI
     buttons. Two rows of three — six in a line is a menu — with each scrap tilted a hair off
     true so the six of them look torn out rather than typeset. */
  .bg-hand {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap: 11px 9px;
    max-width: 400px; margin: 0 auto 6px;
  }
  /* The button is only the hand that holds the scrap: it carries the tilt and the shadow, and
     the paper inside it carries the tear (an inline clip-path, generated per card by
     scrapTear). That split is load-bearing. A drop shadow and a clip-path on ONE element clip
     the shadow away with the corners, which left the old scraps lying flat on the page with no
     shadow at all; on the parent, the shadow follows the torn edge it is cast by. */
  .bg-scrap {
    position: relative; display: block; min-width: 0; padding: 0; cursor: pointer;
    color: var(--ink); background: none; border: 0;
    transform: rotate(var(--scrap-tilt, 0deg));
    filter: drop-shadow(0 1px 0 rgba(43,39,34,0.2)) drop-shadow(0 2px 3px rgba(43,39,34,0.12));
    transition: transform 0.12s;
  }
  .bg-scrap:nth-child(3n+1) { --scrap-tilt: -1.1deg; }
  .bg-scrap:nth-child(3n+2) { --scrap-tilt: 0.6deg; }
  .bg-scrap:nth-child(3n)   { --scrap-tilt: 1.3deg; }
  .bg-scrap-paper {
    display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: stretch; justify-content: center; gap: 1px;
    height: 58px; overflow: hidden;
    background: var(--scrap-stock, #fbf6ea);
    transition: background-color 0.14s;
  }
  .bg-scrap:hover:not(:disabled) .bg-scrap-paper { background-color: var(--scrap-hi, #fff8e6); }
  .bg-scrap:active:not(:disabled) { transform: rotate(var(--scrap-tilt, 0deg)) translateY(1px); }
  .bg-scrap:focus-visible { outline: none; }
  .bg-scrap:focus-visible .bg-scrap-paper { box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 2px var(--ink-accent); }
  /* Torn paper scraps carrying tokenised writing: the same card-and-ink pairing as the
     personal-best tiles, so the same dark stock. Left alone, the word on the scrap turned
     cream on a cream tear and the whole hand went blank. */
  :root[data-theme="dark"] .bg-scrap {
    --scrap-stock: #3a3226; --scrap-hi: #473d2e; --scrap-rule: rgba(236,229,214,0.13);
    filter: drop-shadow(0 1px 0 rgba(0,0,0,0.35)) drop-shadow(0 2px 3px rgba(0,0,0,0.25));
  }
  /* The line the word was cut from: the word in the middle column at its own width, and what
     the line sang either side of it filling the rest, in pencil, running off under the tear.
     The left run is pushed against the word (flex-end) so it is the START of the line that goes
     under the edge and the letters touching the word that survive, which is how a pair of
     scissors would have left it. The sheet's rule runs under the whole line, at the baseline. */
  .bg-scrap-line {
    position: relative; display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr auto 1fr; align-items: baseline;
    font-family: var(--hand); font-size: calc(21px + var(--text-size-adjust)); line-height: 1.24;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .bg-scrap-line::after {
    content: ""; position: absolute; left: 0; right: 0; bottom: 0.19em; height: 1px;
    background: var(--scrap-rule, rgba(43,39,34,0.12));
  }
  .bg-scrap-cut { display: flex; min-width: 0; overflow: hidden; color: var(--ink-soft); opacity: 0.42; }
  .bg-scrap-cut.is-pre { justify-content: flex-end; }
  .bg-scrap-cut > span { flex: none; white-space: pre; }
  .bg-scrap-word {
    position: relative; z-index: 1; padding: 0 0.18em;
    font-family: var(--hand); font-weight: 700; color: var(--ink);
  }
  /* The reveal, written on the scrap itself: what the catalogue really thinks of that word. */
  .bg-scrap-count {
    text-align: center; line-height: 1;
    font-family: var(--type); font-size: calc(8.5px + var(--text-size-adjust)); letter-spacing: 1.1px; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  /* The rarest in the hand, gone over in gold with the round screen's chisel highlighter (the
     stroke itself is the shared rule, named there), so the perfect play is visible on every
     page including one played badly. It is marked whether or not it was picked — that is the
     whole reveal. Gold rather than the era's highlighter, because gold is what "the right card"
     means everywhere on the shelf. */
  .bg-scrap.is-best .bg-scrap-word { --hl: rgba(240, 190, 50, 0.6); }
  .bg-scrap.is-best .bg-scrap-count { color: #8a6a17; }
  :root[data-theme="dark"] .bg-scrap.is-best .bg-scrap-word { --hl: rgba(214, 165, 40, 0.5); }
  :root[data-theme="dark"] .bg-scrap.is-best .bg-scrap-count { color: #e0b54a; }
  /* The card the panel underneath is about, lifted a little off the page. Lift and a deeper
     shadow, no outline: an outline would be a UI selection box drawn round a piece of paper. */
  .bg-scrap.is-focus {
    transform: rotate(var(--scrap-tilt, 0deg)) translateY(-3px);
    filter: drop-shadow(0 1px 0 rgba(43,39,34,0.18)) drop-shadow(0 5px 6px rgba(43,39,34,0.18));
  }
  :root[data-theme="dark"] .bg-scrap.is-focus {
    filter: drop-shadow(0 1px 0 rgba(0,0,0,0.35)) drop-shadow(0 5px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.45));
  }
  /* The player's own card, marked in felt tip OVER the scrap's corner rather than inside it:
     the mark was made on the page after the scrap was laid down, so it runs off the edge. */
  .bg-scrap-mark { position: absolute; top: -10px; right: -8px; z-index: 2; pointer-events: none; }
  .bg-scrap-mark .vmark { display: block; width: 24px; height: 24px; }
  .bg-scrap-mark .vmark-good { width: 27px; height: 27px; }

  /* ---------- Aaron or Jack: the three stamps ----------
     A production credit is a thing STAMPED on a sleeve, so the three answers are three rubber
     stamps: a struck box, a hairline inside it, and the name in spaced capitals. Ink on paper,
     which is what this whole notebook is.

     THEY USED TO BE TORN SCRAPS and that was the reason to change: a scrap is Only Here's card,
     and Only Here is the game immediately to the left of this one on the shelf. Two neighbours
     cannot share an object. Nothing else on the shelf is a stamp.

     THE INKS ARE THE COVER'S, a shade deeper. The cover's teal and vermillion are chosen to read
     as paper at 24px; type knocked out of them at 15px needs more weight behind it, so these are
     the same two hues pushed down rather than a second palette. Do not let the two drift apart —
     the whole page is one idea printed twice, on the collage and on the controls. */
  .bg-who-row {
    --who-teal: #2f7f95;
    --who-verm: #c9552a;
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap: 10px;
    max-width: 430px; margin: 4px auto 8px;
  }
  .bg-who {
    background: none; border: 0; padding: 0; min-height: 78px; cursor: pointer;
    display: grid; place-items: center; transition: opacity 0.14s;
  }
  /* Three different tilts, because three stamps struck by hand are not three stamps aligned. */
  .bg-who[data-v="aaron"] { transform: rotate(-1.4deg); color: var(--who-teal); }
  .bg-who[data-v="both"]  { transform: rotate(0.5deg);  color: var(--who-teal); }
  .bg-who[data-v="jack"]  { transform: rotate(1.2deg);  color: var(--who-verm); }
  .bg-who-stamp {
    position: relative; display: grid; place-items: center; padding: 13px 14px;
    border: 2.5px solid currentColor; border-radius: 2px; transition: transform 0.1s;
  }
  .bg-who-stamp::after {
    content: ""; position: absolute; inset: 3px; border: 1px solid currentColor;
    border-radius: 1px; opacity: 0.5;
  }
  .bg-who-word {
    position: relative; z-index: 2; font-family: var(--type); font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.15em; line-height: 1;
    font-size: calc(15px + var(--text-size-adjust));
  }
  /* THE SECOND STRIKE, and it is the BOX that is struck twice rather than the word. Doubling the
     word was the first idea and is unreadable at this size — two four-letter words a few pixels
     apart stop being one word printed twice and become a scribble. Striking the frame twice says
     the same thing and leaves the name legible. `multiply` is what makes the crossing darken on
     its own, so the overlap is a real overprint rather than a third colour anybody picked; it is
     a blend of two flat colours, not a gradient, so it stays inside the ink-on-paper rule. */
  .bg-who-strike {
    position: absolute; inset: 0; border: 2.5px solid var(--who-verm); border-radius: 2px;
    mix-blend-mode: multiply; transform: translate(5px, 4px); pointer-events: none;
  }
  .bg-who[data-v="both"] .bg-who-stamp::after { display: none; }

  /* THE REVEAL: the true stamp is the one that actually took the ink — filled solid, name knocked
     out in paper — while the others stay as light impressions. There is NO GOLD anywhere here and
     that is deliberate. A gold ring was tried and it is a third frame on a card that already has
     two; the shelf's own reveal idiom is a gold WASH (Only Here's best card), and a wash has
     nothing to do on a stamp that is about to be filled anyway. "This one printed" is a reason
     rather than a styling, which is why it survives on all three. */
  .bg-who.is-answer .bg-who-stamp { background: currentColor; }
  .bg-who.is-answer .bg-who-stamp::after { border-color: var(--paper); opacity: 0.3; }
  .bg-who.is-answer .bg-who-word { color: var(--paper); }
  /* "both" PRINTS IN BOTH INKS, split straight down the middle, and the split is VERTICAL for two
     reasons. The boundary runs perpendicular to the word, so it falls between the O and the T and
     every letter sits wholly in one ink — a diagonal cuts through the letterforms instead. And
     the row reads Aaron | both | Jack, so teal lands on the left where Aaron's stamp is and
     vermillion on the right where Jack's is: the card is its two neighbours pushed together. A
     diagonal throws that mapping away for decoration.
     TWO BLOCKS RATHER THAN A GRADIENT. A hard-stop linear-gradient renders identically, but the
     house rule is no gradients in page art and a rule worth having is worth keeping literally. */
  .bg-who-half { position: absolute; top: 0; bottom: 0; width: 50%; display: none; }
  .bg-who-half.is-a { left: 0; background: var(--who-teal); }
  .bg-who-half.is-j { right: 0; background: var(--who-verm); }
  .bg-who[data-v="both"].is-answer .bg-who-stamp { background: none; border-color: #6a4a4a; }
  .bg-who[data-v="both"].is-answer .bg-who-strike { display: none; }
  .bg-who[data-v="both"].is-answer .bg-who-half { display: block; }

  /* A wrong pick fades rather than being struck through: the stamp it should have been is right
     beside it, and two marks competing on one row is noise. */
  .bg-who.is-missed { opacity: 0.5; }
  .bg-who:disabled { cursor: default; }
  .bg-who:disabled:not(.is-answer):not(.is-missed) { opacity: 0.55; }
  /* The press, on the stamp rather than the button, so it does not cancel the button's tilt. */
  .bg-who:active:not(:disabled) .bg-who-stamp { transform: translateY(1px); }

  /* Dark stock takes the inks up, or two mid-tone inks on a near-black page go muddy. */
  :root[data-theme="dark"] .bg-who-row { --who-teal: #5fb6cc; --who-verm: #e97a4d; }
  :root[data-theme="dark"] .bg-who[data-v="both"].is-answer .bg-who-stamp { border-color: #9a7f6a; }

  /* ---- Who Held The Pen: one, two or three pens ----
     NOT A STAMP AND NOT A SCRAP. Aaron or Jack's three cards are rubber stamps and Only Here's
     are torn scraps, and those two games sit within one tile of this one on the shelf — a third
     set borrowing either silhouette would turn three neighbours into one object drawn three
     times. So these cards are the instruments themselves: one, two or three pens standing on
     the card, each a different kind. The picture carries the question on its own, so the word
     underneath is a caption and is set small.

     WHY NOT MARKS. The first build drew one, two or three written flourishes, and the count was
     a tally of three near-identical squiggles — "and one" and "a room" took a beat to tell
     apart, which is the one thing a three-card page cannot afford. Three UNLIKE objects are
     read instead of counted. Two other families reached the last round and were ruled out on
     collisions rather than taste, and both are worth remembering before anybody reopens this:
     three ruled lines with one, two or all three signed made an empty line mean two different
     things, since a blank line is already how the reveal says "not the answer"; and tally
     strokes, which are the most legible marks of the lot, are exactly `.life-mark`, the round
     screen's lives tally standing in the margin. The board is at scripts/bonus/pen-cards.html.

     See penHandMarkup in app.js for the drawing and for why the first pen never changes. */
  /* ---------- NASHVILLE: two doors and a way out ----------
     A PAIR AND AN ESCAPE, not three of a kind, and the shape is the argument. Hers and not-hers
     are matched blocks of one width, because they are the two halves of one question. PASS is
     smaller, set below them and drawn as a printer's ornament rather than a block, because it
     is not a third guess — it is the decision to make none, and a third equal block would give a
     shrug the same weight as a call. The whole point of the door is that using it well is a
     skill, and the page has to say that before the player has read the rules.

     THE DOORS ARE A HATCH SHOW PRINT POSTER, which is Nashville's own letterpress shop and the
     most 2006-country object there is that is still ink on paper: a heavy rule round each block
     pulled twice a hair off register, three stars over slab capitals (Courier Prime bold IS a
     slab serif, so no poster face was added for it), a double rule under. The pass door is the
     pointing fist out of the same type case, sending you on to the next page. The board these
     were picked from is scripts/bonus/nashville-buttons.html.

     THE INK IS THE COVER'S. The zine is a bone cassette on a hot rust ground, so the page is
     laid in that rust — Aaron or Jack's rule, where the tap cards carry the arithmetic of the
     venn so the page teaches what the picture meant. */
  /* The neutral verdict: neither the good banner's gold nor the bad one's red, because a
     passed page was neither. */
  .banner.pass { color: var(--ink-soft); opacity: 0.82; }
  .bg-feedback.pass { --bg-fb: var(--ink-soft); }

  .bg-nash-row, .bg-nash-pass { --nash-ink: #9c4a22; --nash-wash: rgba(156, 74, 34, 0.08); }
  :root[data-theme="dark"] :is(.bg-nash-row, .bg-nash-pass) { --nash-ink: #d0804f; --nash-wash: rgba(208, 128, 79, 0.1); }
  .bg-nash-row {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); gap: 14px;
    max-width: 400px; margin: 4px auto 6px;
  }
  .bg-nash {
    background: none; border: 0; padding: 0; min-height: 64px; cursor: pointer;
    display: grid; place-items: stretch; transition: opacity 0.14s;
  }
  /* Two blocks laid down by hand, so two different tilts and neither the negative of the other —
     the ornament rule everywhere else in the notebook. */
  .bg-nash[data-v="hers"] { transform: rotate(-1.1deg); }
  .bg-nash[data-v="not"]  { transform: rotate(0.8deg); }
  .bg-nash-card {
    position: relative; width: 100%; box-sizing: border-box;
    display: grid; gap: 3px; justify-items: center; align-content: center; padding: 9px 6px 8px;
    border: 3px solid var(--nash-ink); background: none; transition: transform 0.1s, background-color 0.14s;
  }
  /* The press's second pull, a hair off register. Flat and faint rather than a shadow: it is
     ink that landed in the wrong place, not light falling on an object. */
  .bg-nash-card::after {
    content: ""; position: absolute; inset: -3px; border: 3px solid var(--nash-ink);
    opacity: 0.28; transform: translate(2.5px, 2px); pointer-events: none;
  }
  .bg-nash-stars { display: flex; justify-content: center; gap: 5px; color: var(--nash-ink); opacity: 0.85; }
  .bg-nash-stars svg { display: block; width: 11px; height: 11px; }
  /* Typewriter capitals, like Aaron or Jack's stamps and for the same reason: it is a label on
     a poster rather than something written by hand, so it wants no entry in ink.css's three
     var(--hand) lists. */
  .bg-nash-word {
    font-family: var(--type); font-weight: 700; font-size: 24px; letter-spacing: 3px; line-height: 1;
    text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--nash-ink);
  }
  .bg-nash[data-v="not"] .bg-nash-word { font-size: 20px; letter-spacing: 2px; }
  .bg-nash-rules { width: 82%; height: 5px; border-top: 1.5px solid var(--nash-ink); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--nash-ink); opacity: 0.7; }
  .bg-nash:not([disabled]):hover .bg-nash-card { background: var(--nash-wash); }
  .bg-nash:not([disabled]):active .bg-nash-card { transform: translate(1px, 1px); }
  /* THE TRUE DOOR TOOK THE INK and the other is left as an impression — Aaron or Jack's "this
     one printed", said again deliberately rather than invented a fourth way. On a poster block
     it is also literally what printing does: the type knocks out of the solid. */
  .bg-nash.is-answer .bg-nash-card { background: var(--nash-ink); }
  .bg-nash.is-answer :is(.bg-nash-word, .bg-nash-stars) { color: var(--paper); }
  .bg-nash.is-answer .bg-nash-rules { border-color: var(--paper); }
  .bg-nash.is-blank .bg-nash-card { opacity: 0.35; }
  .bg-nash.is-missed .bg-nash-card { border-color: var(--bad, #b4453a); border-style: dashed; }
  /* THE CALL IS PRINTED INTO THE BLOCK, and that is the only thing that tells a right call from
     a pass: both end with the true block inked, because the ink is the truth and is owed either
     way. It is a small tick knocked out of the solid in paper beside the stars, as though the
     printer had set it in the forme, so nothing is drawn over the poster. It replaced a red pen
     ring round the block; the board it was picked from is scripts/bonus/nashville-called.html. */
  .bg-nash-tick { position: absolute; right: 8px; top: 6px; width: 17px; height: 15px; color: var(--paper); pointer-events: none; }
  .bg-nash-tick path { fill: none; stroke: currentColor; stroke-width: 2.4; stroke-linecap: round; stroke-linejoin: round; }
  /* The way out: the printer's fist under the pair, never a third block. It leans toward the
     next page on hover and fills in when it is taken; the other pass states just step back. */
  .bg-nash-pass {
    display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; width: max-content; margin: 10px auto 8px; padding: 2px 6px;
    background: none; border: 0; color: var(--nash-ink);
    font-family: var(--type); font-weight: 700; font-size: 13px; letter-spacing: 3px; text-transform: uppercase;
    opacity: 0.8; cursor: pointer; transition: opacity 0.14s;
  }
  .bg-nash-fist { width: 38px; height: 17px; overflow: visible; transition: transform 0.15s; }
  .bg-nash-fist path { fill: none; stroke: currentColor; stroke-width: 1.5; stroke-linejoin: round; stroke-linecap: round; }
  .bg-nash-fist .cuff { fill: currentColor; stroke: none; }
  .bg-nash-pass:not([disabled]):hover { opacity: 1; }
  .bg-nash-pass:not([disabled]):hover .bg-nash-fist { transform: translateX(3px); }
  .bg-nash-pass[disabled] { cursor: default; opacity: 0.3; }
  .bg-nash-pass.is-took { opacity: 1; }
  .bg-nash-pass.is-took .hand { fill: currentColor; }
  .bg-nash-pass.is-took .knuck { stroke: var(--paper); }
  @media (max-width: 420px) {
    .bg-nash-row { gap: 10px; }
    .bg-nash-word { font-size: 20px; letter-spacing: 2px; }
    .bg-nash[data-v="not"] .bg-nash-word { font-size: 16px; letter-spacing: 1.4px; }
  }

  .bg-pen-row {
    /* The cover's own ink, so the page and the collage agree — Aaron or Jack's rule, where the
       three tap cards carry the arithmetic of the venn so the page teaches what the picture
       meant. A signature here is written in the same oxblood the blot is. */
    --pen-ink: #7a2230;
    --pen-pencil: rgba(60, 40, 18, 0.42);
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap: 10px;
    max-width: 460px; margin: 4px auto 8px;
  }
  .bg-pen {
    background: none; border: 0; padding: 0; min-height: 78px; cursor: pointer;
    display: grid; place-items: stretch; transition: opacity 0.14s;
  }
  /* Three slips laid down by hand, so three different tilts — and none of them the negative of
     another, which is the ornament rule everywhere else in the notebook. */
  .bg-pen[data-v="solo"] { transform: rotate(-1.3deg); }
  .bg-pen[data-v="one"]  { transform: rotate(0.6deg); }
  .bg-pen[data-v="room"] { transform: rotate(1.5deg); }
  /* THE THREE SLIPS ARE ONE WIDTH, and it has to be forced: the row is three equal grid
     columns, but a shrink-to-fit slip inside them came out 57px, 79px and 111px, because a
     card with three pens on it is wider than a card with one. Three different-sized cards do
     not read as a set of three answers — they read as three unrelated things, and the widest
     one looks like the important one. So the slip fills its column and the pens centre in it. */
  .bg-pen-slip {
    width: 100%; box-sizing: border-box;
    display: grid; justify-items: center; gap: 2px; padding: 8px 8px 6px;
    border: 1.5px solid rgba(60, 40, 18, 0.22); border-radius: 2px;
    background: rgba(255, 252, 243, 0.5); transition: transform 0.1s, border-color 0.14s;
  }
  :root[data-theme="dark"] .bg-pen-slip { background: rgba(255, 252, 243, 0.05); }
  /* The glyphs are the main game's own pens (PEN_SVG), so these rules mirror `.pen-glyph`'s
     with two changes. Everything is `currentColor` instead of `--ink-soft`, so one property
     moves the whole row from pencil to ink at the reveal; and the band, grip and the gel pen's
     sparks come in on that same colour rather than on `--ink-accent` and `--bead`. The colour
     is dropped deliberately: a gold spark on the third card only would pull the eye to "a room"
     before the player had read the title, which is the leak the page avoids everywhere else. */
  .bg-pen-hand {
    display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; gap: 3px;
    width: 100%; height: 34px; color: var(--pen-pencil);
  }
  .bg-pen-glyph { display: block; width: 28px; height: 28px; }
  .bg-pen-glyph svg { width: 100%; height: 100%; overflow: visible; }
  .bg-pen-hand .barrel, .bg-pen-hand .vane {
    fill: var(--paper); stroke: currentColor; stroke-width: 1.2; stroke-linejoin: round;
  }
  .bg-pen-hand .nib, .bg-pen-hand .tip,
  .bg-pen-hand .band, .bg-pen-hand .grip, .bg-pen-hand .glitter-spark { fill: currentColor; stroke: none; }
  .bg-pen-hand .spine { fill: none; stroke: currentColor; stroke-width: 1.1; stroke-linecap: round; }
  .bg-pen-hand .barb { fill: none; stroke: currentColor; stroke-width: 0.7; stroke-linecap: round; opacity: 0.75; }
  .bg-pen-hand .slit { fill: none; stroke: var(--paper); stroke-width: 0.7; stroke-linecap: round; }
  .bg-pen-hand .hole { fill: var(--paper); stroke: none; }
  .bg-pen-word {
    font-family: var(--type); text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.13em;
    font-size: calc(10px + var(--text-size-adjust)); color: var(--ink-soft); line-height: 1;
  }

  /* THE REVEAL: the true line is the one that got SIGNED, and the other two are left blank.
     That is Aaron or Jack's "this one printed" said in this game's own object instead of
     borrowed from it — there the true stamp is the one that took the ink, here the true line is
     the one somebody wrote on. It needs no gold and no cross for the same reason that one
     doesn't: the mark is the answer, so the answer needs no second mark on top of it. */
  .bg-pen.is-answer .bg-pen-slip { border-color: var(--pen-ink); }
  .bg-pen.is-answer .bg-pen-hand { color: var(--pen-ink); }
  .bg-pen.is-answer .bg-pen-word { color: var(--pen-ink); font-weight: 700; }
  /* GHOSTED, NOT EMPTIED. The pens that were not picked up fade almost out rather than
     vanishing: an empty card with no object on it reads as a rendering fault, where a faint
     pen reads as the answer it wasn't. It is also why nothing is crossed through — the inked
     card is right beside it, and two marks competing on one row is noise. */
  .bg-pen.is-blank .bg-pen-ink { opacity: 0.2; }
  .bg-pen.is-missed { opacity: 0.55; }
  .bg-pen:disabled { cursor: default; }
  .bg-pen:disabled:not(.is-answer):not(.is-missed) { opacity: 0.6; }
  .bg-pen:active:not(:disabled) .bg-pen-slip { transform: translateY(1px); }
  .bg-pen:not(:disabled):hover .bg-pen-slip { border-color: rgba(60, 40, 18, 0.45); }

  /* Oxblood on near-black stock goes to a dusty rose, or the signed line is a hole in the page. */
  :root[data-theme="dark"] .bg-pen-row { --pen-ink: #d4808a; --pen-pencil: rgba(240, 232, 214, 0.38); }

  /* The panel under a turned-over hand: one card at a time, opening on the rarest (see
     focusOnlyCard). The count in the round screen's typed label with its songs as dots beside
     it, the song drawn with the card's lines lit, and the line the word lives in. It goes under
     the hand rather than under the heading, because by then the hand is the answer key and
     this is the footnote to whichever card you are reading. */
  .bg-only-reveal { text-align: center; margin: 14px auto 16px; max-width: 420px; }
  .bg-only-said {
    display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; flex-wrap: wrap; column-gap: 12px; row-gap: 6px;
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--type); font-size: calc(10.5px + var(--text-size-adjust)); letter-spacing: 1.5px;
    text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .bg-only-said b { color: var(--ink); }
  .bg-only-say { text-wrap: balance; }
  .bg-only-said.is-below { flex-direction: column; }
  .bg-only-wave { display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; gap: 10px; margin: 8px 0 0; }
  .bg-only-line {
    font-family: var(--hand); font-size: calc(20px + var(--text-size-adjust)); line-height: 1.3;
    color: var(--ink-soft); margin: 5px 0 0;
  }

  /* Redacted — a verse with its telling words under strips of correction tape.
     The word sits in the DOM under the strip rather than being left out, which is what makes
     every block exactly its own word wide (no guessing at letter widths in a handwriting
     face) and what lets a strip lift OFF something instead of a blank appearing. */
  .bg-redact {
    display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center; gap: 3px;
    max-width: min(54ch, 100%); margin: 0 auto 14px;
  }
  .bg-rline {
    display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; justify-content: center; align-items: baseline; gap: 1px 7px;
  }
  .bg-tok { font-family: var(--hand); font-size: calc(24px + var(--text-size-adjust)); line-height: 1.35; color: var(--ink); }
  .bg-tape {
    position: relative; display: inline-block; font: inherit; color: inherit;
    background: none; border: 0; padding: 0; margin: 0; cursor: pointer;
  }
  .bg-tape-word { visibility: hidden; }
  .bg-tape.is-open { cursor: default; }
  .bg-tape.is-open .bg-tape-word { visibility: visible; }
  /* Left taped to the end: the word comes back under a faint wash, so the finished verse
     also reads as a record of how much of it was guessed at blind. */
  .bg-tape.was-taped .bg-tape-word {
    background: linear-gradient(rgba(199,149,31,0.16), rgba(199,149,31,0.16));
  }
  /* The strip itself. Correction tape, not marker ink: a flat opaque off-white a shade cooler
     than the paper, laid over the word with a soft shadow so it reads as sitting ON the page,
     and a slightly darker edge where the roller pressed it down. */
  .bg-tape-strip {
    position: absolute; left: -3px; right: -3px; top: 0.14em; bottom: 0.2em;
    background:
      linear-gradient(180deg, rgba(255,255,255,0.55), rgba(0,0,0,0.045) 62%, rgba(0,0,0,0.02)),
      #f2eee2;
    box-shadow: 0 1px 1.5px rgba(43,39,34,0.22), inset 0 0 0 0.5px rgba(43,39,34,0.14);
    border-radius: 1px; transform-origin: 8% 60%;
  }
  .bg-tape:hover:not(.is-open) .bg-tape-strip { background-color: #f7f4ea; }
  .bg-tape:active:not(.is-open) .bg-tape-strip { transform: translateY(1px); }
  /* The peel: the strip lifts from its left edge and flicks away off the page. */
  .bg-tape-strip.is-peeling { animation: bgPeel 0.34s ease-in forwards; pointer-events: none; }
  @keyframes bgPeel {
    0%   { transform: none; opacity: 1; }
    45%  { transform: rotate(-13deg) translate(1px, -3px); opacity: 1; }
    100% { transform: rotate(-34deg) translate(7px, -19px); opacity: 0; }
  }
  /* What the page still pays, in the typewriter voice the rest of the chrome uses. */
  .bg-worth {
    font-family: var(--type); font-size: calc(11px + var(--text-size-adjust)); letter-spacing: 1.2px; text-transform: lowercase;
    color: var(--ink-soft); text-align: center; margin: 0 0 18px;
  }
  .bg-worth b { font-size: calc(15px + var(--text-size-adjust)); color: var(--ink); display: inline-block; }
  .bg-worth b.is-spent { animation: bgSpend 0.32s ease; }
  @keyframes bgSpend {
    0% { transform: none; color: var(--margin); }
    40% { transform: translateY(-3px) scale(1.14); color: var(--margin); }
    100% { transform: none; }
  }

  /* Ruthless Game — the song writing itself out, a word a second, against the red margin rule.
     Handwriting rather than .bg-lyric's quoted treatment, and left-aligned: this is a sheet
     being filled in as you watch, not a line held up for inspection.
     The FIXED height is load-bearing rather than cosmetic. A page can run to fifty or sixty
     words, and a box that grew with them would walk the writing line down the screen while the
     player was typing into it — so the box is a window the stream scrolls inside (kept at the
     bottom by revealRuthlessWord), and nothing under it ever moves. */
  .bg-stream {
    font-family: var(--hand); font-size: calc(22px + var(--text-size-adjust)); line-height: 1.5; color: var(--ink);
    max-width: 34ch; margin: 0 auto 14px; padding: 2px 12px 2px 14px; text-align: left;
    height: 8.5em; overflow-y: auto; overscroll-behavior: contain;
    border-left: 2px solid rgba(178,58,63,0.35);
  }
  /* Each word arrives as its own inline-block so it can be animated in on its own; the margin
     is what spaces them, since the DOM has no whitespace between them to collapse. */
  .bg-drip { display: inline-block; margin-right: 0.3em; }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    .bg-drip { animation: bgDrip 0.26s ease both; }
    @keyframes bgDrip { from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(-3px); } to { opacity: 1; transform: none; } }
  }
  /* Answered: the page stops being the question and becomes the lyric sheet under its heading. */
  .bg-stream.is-done { opacity: 0.78; border-left-color: rgba(43,39,34,0.18); }
  /* ---- Word Cloud ----
     The words are absolutely positioned by js/cloud.js, which has already measured the real
     glyphs, fitted the whole block to this box's width and hung every word off the box's own
     centre line, so there is almost nothing for CSS to do here and it must not try: any padding, letter-spacing or font-weight set on the word
     below would change the metrics the packing was computed from and open gaps the ink masks
     closed. The container's HEIGHT is written by the painter for the same reason. */
  .bg-cloud {
    position: relative; width: 100%; margin: 2px auto 14px;
    /* A cloud fitted to a stale width (a turned phone, mid-repaint) is clipped rather than
       allowed to run out over the writing line underneath it. */
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .bg-cloud-word {
    position: absolute; font-family: var(--hand); font-weight: 600; line-height: 1;
    color: var(--ink); white-space: nowrap; transform-origin: center;
    -webkit-user-select: none; user-select: none;
  }
  .bg-giveup { display: flex; width: max-content; max-width: 100%; margin: 12px auto 0; }
  /* Walking away is paid for, so give up is a penalty ticket, notched either side, with the
     fine printed on its stub. Grey, and counting the words still to come, until it can be
     taken; then it goes red pen and names what it adds to the clock. */
  .giveup-ticket {
    align-items: stretch; padding: 0;
    font-size: calc(12px + var(--text-size-adjust)); font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: 1px; color: var(--red-pen);
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--red-pen) 16%, var(--paper)); border: 2px solid currentColor; border-radius: 3px;
    -webkit-mask: radial-gradient(circle 6px at 0 50%, #0000 97%, #000) left / 51% 100% no-repeat, radial-gradient(circle 6px at 100% 50%, #0000 97%, #000) right / 51% 100% no-repeat;
    mask: radial-gradient(circle 6px at 0 50%, #0000 97%, #000) left / 51% 100% no-repeat, radial-gradient(circle 6px at 100% 50%, #0000 97%, #000) right / 51% 100% no-repeat;
    transition: background 0.14s;
  }
  .giveup-ticket > span { padding: 8px 14px 8px 17px; }
  .giveup-ticket > b { padding: 8px 17px 8px 12px; border-left: 1.5px solid currentColor; letter-spacing: 0.5px; }
  .giveup-ticket:not(:disabled):is(:hover, :focus-visible) { background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--red-pen) 26%, var(--paper)); }
  .giveup-ticket:disabled { cursor: default; opacity: 0.7; color: var(--ink-soft); background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink) 8%, var(--paper)); }
  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .bg-stream { font-size: calc(20px + var(--text-size-adjust)); height: 8em; }
  }

  /* Name That Song — the line, quoted like a copied-out lyric. */
  .bg-lyric {
    font-family: var(--hand); font-size: calc(27px + var(--text-size-adjust)); line-height: 1.35; color: var(--ink);
    text-align: center; max-width: 30ch; margin: 0 auto 24px; padding: 0 10px;
    border-left: 2px solid rgba(178,58,63,0.35);
  }

  /* Running Order asks its question in a `bg-sheet`'s TITLE slot, so it needs almost nothing of
     its own: a little more air under the heading than a reveal wants, since here the heading is
     the thing being read rather than the answer to something above it. Its meta line is empty
     while the page is live and fills in at the reveal, which is why the block still needs its
     bottom margin rather than borrowing one from a line that is not there yet. */
  /* Wider than a reveal's measure. `bg-sheet`'s 34ch is a reading width for a heading with a
     lyric under it; here the heading is set half again as big and has nothing under it, so at
     that measure ordinary titles ("I Can Do It With a Broken Heart") broke over two lines for
     no reason. At this width all but the three longest in the catalogue sit on one line, and
     those three are long enough to have earned the second one, which `balance` splits evenly
     rather than leaving a single word stranded. */
  .bg-sheet--ask { margin: 12px auto 30px; max-width: 46ch; }
  /* Bigger than a reveal's heading, because here it IS the page: one written line with nothing
     else on the paper, which at the shared size reads as a caption for something missing rather
     than as the question. The real title inherits the size when it lands. */
  .bg-sheet--ask .bg-sheet-title { font-size: calc(34px + var(--text-size-adjust)); text-wrap: balance; }
  /* A missed title is written in the editor's red pen (--red-pen), NOT in --margin: that one is
     the printed rule, deliberately faint because it is meant to sit under text, and a heading
     set in it reads as washed out rather than as corrected. A named title gets no mark at all,
     since the banner directly above it has already said so, and a second signal on the same
     fact is the clutter this page was rebuilt to lose. */
  .bg-sheet-title.is-answer { color: var(--red-pen); }

  /* ---------- THE CAPITALS: the booklet's line, quoted ----------
     The question stands in the `bg-sheet--ask` heading slot Running Order's does, so it inherits
     the size and the balance and needs nothing but its quotation marks — which are the page's
     only furniture and are load-bearing rather than decorative. Four of the seventy-two
     messages are a single proper noun ("Toby.", "SAG.") and a bare word in a heading slot reads
     as a TITLE, which is the one thing this page must never look like it is handing over. The
     marks say the line is quoted from somewhere else.
     They are drawn HERE rather than typed into the string so that a message already ending in a
     full stop — most of them do — is never re-punctuated by the markup, and so the reveal can
     drop the claim by dropping one class when the slot stops holding a quote. */
  .bg-caps-note::before { content: "\201C"; }
  .bg-caps-note::after  { content: "\201D"; }
  .bg-caps-note::before, .bg-caps-note::after { color: var(--margin); font-weight: 400; }
  /* NO SIZE STEP FOR A LONG MESSAGE, and it is not an omission — see renderBonusRound. The
     longest in the pool balances to two lines here and four on a phone with the writing line
     still well above the fold, so the only thing a shrink would buy is a smaller page for the
     best message on the record. */
  /* Where the message goes once the reveal has taken its slot: under the finished heading, in
     the same quoted hand but small and soft, so the page ends as a lyric sheet with the
     booklet's line noted beneath it. Running Order had nothing to keep here — a track number is
     a coordinate — but a message and its song are a PAIR, and the pairing is the thing the game
     is teaching, so it must not leave the screen at the moment it is finally worth reading. */
  .bg-caps-said {
    font-family: var(--hand); font-size: calc(15px + var(--text-size-adjust)); line-height: 1.25;
    color: var(--ink-soft); text-align: center; max-width: 34ch; margin: -8px auto 18px;
  }
  .bg-caps-said:empty { display: none; margin: 0; }
  .bg-caps-said::before { content: "\201C"; }
  .bg-caps-said::after  { content: "\201D"; }

  .bg-reject {
    opacity: 0; font-family: var(--type); font-size: calc(10.5px + var(--text-size-adjust)); letter-spacing: 0.8px;
    color: var(--margin); text-align: center; pointer-events: none;
  }
  .bg-reject.show { animation: bgReject 1.1s ease forwards; }
  @keyframes bgReject { 0% { opacity: 0; } 15% { opacity: 1; } 75% { opacity: 1; } 100% { opacity: 0; } }

  /* Verdict, shared by all three games. The parts inside it are the ROUND SCREEN's own — the
     handwritten .banner, the .lyric-card carrying the real lyric, the .countdown to the next
     page — so what belongs here is only the block that holds them: centred, and narrower than
     the notebook page, because a bonus page is a smaller thing than a real one. The card
     itself goes back to reading left-to-right, since a lyric is not a caption. */
  .bg-feedback { display: none; margin: 22px auto 0; max-width: 44ch; text-align: center; }
  .bg-feedback.show { display: block; animation: bgFade 0.24s ease; }
  @keyframes bgFade { from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(4px); } to { opacity: 1; transform: none; } }
  .bg-detail {
    font-family: var(--type); font-size: calc(12.5px + var(--text-size-adjust)); line-height: 1.5; color: var(--ink-soft);
    margin: 0 0 14px;
  }
  .bg-feedback .lyric-card { text-align: left; }
  .bg-feedback .countdown { margin-top: 14px; }

  /* ---------- End of a run: the back cover ----------
     A bonus run keeps nothing but its own best score, so it has no bracelet and no records
     board to be proud of. The keepsake is instead the BACK COVER of the zine it was played
     out of, written up by hand: the cover and the score at the top, then the ten pages listed
     with a tick or a cross in the margin and the answer noted beside each. --bg-tint is the
     game's own colour, set inline, so each back cover belongs to its own booklet. */
  /* THE CARD'S WIDTH, STATED ONCE. The actions under the back cover line up with its edges, so
     the two have to agree — and two numbers that must match are two numbers that eventually
     will not. The row used to be capped narrower than the card, which read as a row of controls
     parked under a card rather than as the card's own bottom edge. */
  .bg-end { padding: 6px 0 4px; --bg-card-w: 560px; }
  .bg-back {
    position: relative; border: 1px solid rgba(43,39,34,0.18); border-radius: 3px;
    background: rgba(251,247,234,0.94); box-shadow: 0 1px 0 rgba(43,39,34,0.06);
    padding: 20px 20px 15px; max-width: var(--bg-card-w); margin: 0 auto;
  }
  /* The back cover is a paper card carrying tokenised writing, so it takes the dark stock the
     personal-best tiles take, and NOT a brightness filter. It looks like a filter case — a
     printed object with its own tint — but almost everything on it, the game's name, the big
     score, every one of the ten page titles, reads --ink or --ink-soft. On the cream card those
     all flipped to cream at night: the run's only keepsake was a blank slab with a faint "/10"
     on it. A filter would have dimmed the blankness rather than fixed it.

     This does not touch the PNG. The saved card is drawn from scratch in js/backcard.js on a
     canvas that cannot see this stylesheet at all — the skill notes say so explicitly, down to
     color-mix() not resolving in there — so what you take off the page stays the printed cream
     card whatever the screen is wearing, which is correct: a keepsake is a thing you printed,
     not a screenshot of the room you printed it in. */
  :root[data-theme="dark"] .bg-back {
    background: #3a3226;
    border-color: rgba(236, 229, 214, 0.14);
    box-shadow: 0 1px 0 rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.28);
  }
  /* The kicker mixes the game's tint DOWN towards ink to sit on cream; on the dark stock it has
     to come up instead. Same mix, other direction, so a game's own tint still identifies it. */
  :root[data-theme="dark"] .bg-back-kicker {
    color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--bg-tint, #4a8c87) 72%, #ece5d6);
  }
  /* washi tape pinning the back cover to the page, tinted to the game (shared .nav-tape surface) */
  .bg-back::before {
    content: ""; position: absolute; top: -9px; left: 50%; width: 62px; height: 17px;
    transform: translateX(-50%) rotate(-2.4deg);
    --tape-color: var(--bg-tint, #c9b27a);
  }
  .bg-back-head {
    position: relative; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 14px;
    padding-bottom: 12px; border-bottom: 1.5px solid rgba(43,39,34,0.16);
  }
  .bg-back-cover { width: 58px; flex: none; }
  .bg-back-cover .zine-cover {
    width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; border-radius: 2px;
    box-shadow: 0 2px 5px rgba(43, 39, 34, 0.3);
  }
  .bg-back-titles { flex: 1; min-width: 0; text-align: left; }
  .bg-back-kicker {
    font-family: var(--type); font-size: calc(8.5px + var(--text-size-adjust)); letter-spacing: 1.8px; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--bg-tint, #4a8c87) 78%, #2b2722);
  }
  .bg-back-name {
    font-family: var(--hand); font-weight: 700; font-size: calc(26px + var(--text-size-adjust)); line-height: 1.05;
    color: var(--ink); margin: 1px 0 0;
  }
  .bg-back-remark { font-family: var(--type); font-size: calc(11px + var(--text-size-adjust)); color: var(--ink-soft); margin-top: 3px; }
  /* Then What's second number, under the remark: not scored, and set like the small print it
     is, but it is the only thing about a run a total out of sixty cannot say. */
  .bg-back-chain {
    font-family: var(--type); font-size: calc(9px + var(--text-size-adjust)); letter-spacing: 1.4px; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-soft); opacity: 0.75; margin-top: 3px;
  }
  .bg-back-score {
    flex: none; font-family: var(--hand); font-weight: 700; font-size: calc(46px + var(--text-size-adjust)); line-height: 0.9;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .bg-back-score span { font-size: calc(19px + var(--text-size-adjust)); color: var(--ink-soft); }
  /* the rubber stamp, pressed across the corner of the card */
  .bg-stamp {
    position: absolute; right: -4px; top: -12px;
    transform: rotate(-7deg);
    font-family: var(--type); font-weight: 700; font-size: calc(8.5px + var(--text-size-adjust)); letter-spacing: 2.2px;
    text-transform: uppercase; font-style: normal; white-space: nowrap;
    /* Stamp red, taken from --red-pen rather than written out, so the ink the page is using is
       the ink the stamp is pressed in — the same swap the guest shelf's COMING SOON took. */
    color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--red-pen) 80%, transparent); padding: 3px 7px;
    border: 1.4px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--red-pen) 65%, transparent); border-radius: 2px;
  }
  /* The heading over the listing, and on a long run the controls that change which ten pages
     are under it. A row rather than a block for that reason alone: the arrows say which ten,
     and the words beside them say which ten, so they are one thing. */
  .bg-back-label {
    font-family: var(--type); font-size: calc(8.5px + var(--text-size-adjust)); letter-spacing: 1.8px; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-soft); margin: 11px 0 6px;
    display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 10px;
  }
  .bg-tracks-nav { margin-left: auto; display: flex; gap: 2px; flex: none; }
  /* Ink on the card, not a control panel on it: no border, no fill, no rounded box. It comes up
     to full strength when you reach for it, the way a pencil mark darkens under the hand, and
     goes flat and unclickable at the ends of the run rather than disappearing — an arrow that
     vanishes at the last page takes the layout with it. */
  .bg-tracks-arrow {
    appearance: none; background: none; border: 0; padding: 2px 4px; margin: 0;
    color: var(--ink-soft); opacity: 0.72; cursor: pointer; line-height: 0;
    transition: opacity 0.14s ease-out, transform 0.14s ease-out;
  }
  .bg-tracks-arrow:hover, .bg-tracks-arrow:focus-visible { opacity: 1; }
  .bg-tracks-arrow:first-child:hover { transform: translateX(-1.5px); }
  .bg-tracks-arrow:last-child:hover { transform: translateX(1.5px); }
  .bg-tracks-arrow:disabled { opacity: 0.22; cursor: default; transform: none; }
  .bg-arrow-svg { width: 15px; height: 15px; display: block; }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .bg-tracks-arrow, .bg-tracks-arrow:hover { transform: none; }
  }
  /* the track listing — two hand-ruled columns on a wide page, one on a narrow one */
  .bg-tracks { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; columns: 2; column-gap: 22px; }
  .bg-track {
    display: flex; align-items: baseline; gap: 6px; break-inside: avoid;
    padding: 3px 0 4px; border-bottom: 1px dotted rgba(43,39,34,0.16);
  }
  .bg-track-n {
    flex: none; width: 1.5em; font-family: var(--type); font-size: calc(9px + var(--text-size-adjust)); color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  /* The title takes the room and the note gives way, since a truncated song name is worse
     than a truncated one-word note. */
  .bg-track-title {
    flex: 1 1 auto; min-width: 0;
    font-family: var(--hand); font-size: calc(16px + var(--text-size-adjust)); line-height: 1.15; color: var(--ink);
    white-space: nowrap; overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis;
  }
  .bg-track-note {
    flex: 0 1 auto; max-width: 10ch;
    font-family: var(--type); font-size: calc(8.5px + var(--text-size-adjust)); letter-spacing: 0.7px;
    text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-soft); text-align: right;
    white-space: nowrap; overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis;
  }
  /* ...EXCEPT when the note is a time. Running Order's column is a number, and a number that
     gives way does not come back shorter, it comes back WRONG: "10.0s" clipped to "10..." reads
     as a different run. It is at most five characters, so holding its width costs the title
     almost nothing. */
  .bg-track-note.is-time { flex: none; max-width: none; }
  .bg-track-mark { flex: none; width: 14px; }
  .bg-mark-svg { width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; }
  .bg-track.no .bg-track-title { color: var(--ink-soft); }
  /* On a missed track the note is the answer you didn't have, so it's written in red pen. */
  .bg-track.no .bg-track-note { color: var(--red-pen); }
  /* Padded both sides by the width of the copy mark in the corner, so the small print stays
     centred on the card rather than centred on what's left of it. */
  .bg-back-foot {
    position: relative; min-height: 28px;
    display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
    font-family: var(--type); font-size: calc(10px + var(--text-size-adjust)); letter-spacing: 0.9px; color: var(--ink-soft);
    margin-top: 9px; text-align: center; padding: 0 34px;
  }
  /* Taking the back cover off the page. Not a worded button under the card but a small mark
     set at the end of its own small print: what it copies is the card it sits in, and
     it should read as part of that object rather than as a control floating beneath it. It
     is taken out of the flow so the footer stays centred on the card, and answers by swapping
     the stacked-sheets glyph for the same gold tick the track listing uses. */
  .bg-back-copy {
    position: absolute; right: 0; top: 0; bottom: 0; margin: auto 0;
    display: inline-flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
    width: 30px; height: 28px; padding: 0;
    border: 1px solid rgba(43,39,34,0.16); border-radius: 4px;
    background: rgba(43,39,34,0.035); color: var(--ink-soft); cursor: pointer;
    transition: color 0.18s ease, background-color 0.18s ease, border-color 0.18s ease, transform 0.1s ease;
  }
  .keepsake-ic {
    width: 17px; height: 17px; display: block;
    fill: none; stroke: currentColor; stroke-width: 1.5;
    stroke-linecap: round; stroke-linejoin: round;
  }
  .keepsake-ic--kept { display: none; }
  .bg-back-copy:hover {
    color: var(--ink);
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--bg-tint, #4a8c87) 14%, transparent);
    border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--bg-tint, #4a8c87) 55%, rgba(43,39,34,0.28));
  }
  .bg-back-copy:active { transform: translateY(1px); }
  .bg-back-copy:focus-visible { outline: 2px dotted rgba(43,39,34,0.5); outline-offset: 2px; }
  .bg-back-copy[data-state="busy"] { opacity: 0.5; }
  .bg-back-copy[data-state="kept"] {
    color: #c7951f; border-color: rgba(199,149,31,0.5); background: rgba(199,149,31,0.12);
  }
  .bg-back-copy[data-state="kept"] .keepsake-ic--copy { display: none; }
  .bg-back-copy[data-state="kept"] .keepsake-ic--kept { display: block; }
  .bg-back-copy[data-state="failed"] {
    color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--red-pen) 85%, transparent);
    border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--red-pen) 40%, transparent);
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--red-pen) 8%, transparent);
  }
  .bg-back-copy[disabled] { cursor: default; }
  [data-reduce-motion="on"] .bg-back-copy { transition: none; }

  /* ---- The run's charms, lying on the desk under the back cover ----
     Outside the card and above the actions (see bonusCharmRow in app.js). It borrows the
     results recap's parts — the typewriter label, the charm mark, the handwritten name in the
     charm's own theme colour — and none of its furniture: no band, no rule, no cap, no fold.
     Centred and held to the card's own width so the card, this row and the actions under it
     read as one column rather than three things that happen to be stacked. */
  .bg-charms { max-width: var(--bg-card-w, 100%); margin: 16px auto 0; text-align: center; }
  .bg-charms-lab { margin: 0 0 8px; }
  .bg-charms-row { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; justify-content: center; align-items: center;
    gap: 6px 18px; list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; }
  .bg-charm { display: inline-flex; min-width: 0; }
  /* One control per charm, the mark and its name together: they are two views of the same
     unlock, so hovering half of it and lighting the other half would be the results band's
     pairing machinery rebuilt for a row four items long. Here they are simply one button. */
  .bg-charm-go { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 7px; min-width: 0;
    background: none; border: none; border-radius: 0; padding: 2px 1px; cursor: pointer;
    transition: transform 0.12s ease; }
  .bg-charm-go:hover { transform: translateY(-2px); }
  .bg-charm-go:focus-visible { outline: 1.5px dotted currentColor; outline-offset: 3px; }
  [data-reduce-motion="on"] .bg-charm-go { transition: none; }
  .bg-charm-ic { width: 26px; height: 26px; flex: none; }
  .bg-charm-ic .charm { width: 100%; height: 100%; display: block; }
  .bg-charm-nm { font-family: var(--hand); font-size: calc(16px + var(--text-size-adjust));
    line-height: 1.2; color: var(--ach-theme); }
  /* Track by Track's ending is a bordered panel rather than a card that gets rasterised, so the
     row goes INSIDE it — there is no PNG here for a line to disagree with. It sits below the
     record's own numbers and above the actions, cut off from the time by a dotted rule so
     the panel does not read as one block of four centred lines. `--bg-card-w` is not set on
     this screen, which is what the fallback above is for. */
  .tbt-end .bg-charms { margin: 0 0 12px; padding-top: 11px;
    border-top: 1px dotted color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink-soft) 32%, transparent); }

  /* The back cover's two-up row, cut from the same cloth as the Challenges / Album Focus /
     Custom rows: half-width coloured-pencil washes under a hand-drawn pattern, with the
     replay on the RIGHT because the shelf is where you came from. The shelf's own pair is
     inked in slate indigo hatched into cross-stitch x's, and a berry rose scalloped like
     the grooves running out to the edge of a record. */
  .bg-end-actions { display: flex; gap: 12px; max-width: var(--bg-card-w); margin: 18px auto 0; }
  .bg-end-actions .btn-primary { flex: 1; width: auto; margin: 0; padding: 11px 10px; font-size: calc(13px + var(--text-size-adjust)); }
  .bg-end-actions .btn-primary:first-child { transform: rotate(0.6deg); }

  :is(#bonusShelfBtn, [data-flip-id="bonusShelfBtn"], #tbtPickBtn, [data-flip-id="tbtPickBtn"]) {
    --btn-wash: rgba(112, 122, 178, 0.28);
    --btn-mark: rgba(66, 76, 136, 0.26);
    background-color: var(--btn-wash);
    background-image:
      linear-gradient(45deg, transparent 46.5%, var(--btn-mark) 46.5% 53.5%, transparent 53.5%),
      linear-gradient(-45deg, transparent 46.5%, var(--btn-mark) 46.5% 53.5%, transparent 53.5%);
    background-size: 11px 11px;
    transition: --btn-wash 0.22s ease, --btn-mark 0.22s ease, color 0.2s ease, transform 0.12s, box-shadow 0.2s;
  }
  :is(#bonusShelfBtn, [data-flip-id="bonusShelfBtn"], #tbtPickBtn, [data-flip-id="tbtPickBtn"]):hover {
    color: var(--paper);
    --btn-wash: #4c5488;
    --btn-mark: rgba(245, 239, 225, 0.26);
  }

  :is(#bonusAgainBtn, [data-flip-id="bonusAgainBtn"], #tbtAgainBtn, [data-flip-id="tbtAgainBtn"]) {
    --btn-wash: rgba(186, 96, 122, 0.28);
    --btn-mark: rgba(158, 62, 90, 0.32);
    background-color: var(--btn-wash);
    background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 50% 100%, transparent 4.6px, var(--btn-mark) 4.7px 5.6px, transparent 5.7px);
    background-size: 13px 7.5px;
    transition: --btn-wash 0.22s ease, --btn-mark 0.22s ease, color 0.2s ease, transform 0.12s, box-shadow 0.2s;
  }
  :is(#bonusAgainBtn, [data-flip-id="bonusAgainBtn"], #tbtAgainBtn, [data-flip-id="tbtAgainBtn"]):hover {
    color: var(--paper);
    --btn-wash: #a8465e;
    --btn-mark: rgba(245, 239, 225, 0.30);
  }
  /* ---- the other side of the same game ----
     A third button changes the arithmetic of the row: at 400px three come out at 125 each,
     which fits every label but leaves nothing around them, so a three-button row is allowed
     wider. It stays well inside the card (560px), because the actions have never run the full
     width of the thing they sit under.
     THE PATTERN IS THE POINT OF THIS FAMILY, not decoration on it. Every button in this row is
     a coloured wash with one flat mark repeated across it, and no two of them may share a mark:
     the shelf wears a cross-hatch, replay wears scallops, and the two faces of this one wear
     rings and dashes. They are drawn out of gradients rather than an image so they take the
     wash with them on hover, when the button floods to full colour and the mark turns to paper.
     Both new marks say what the button does before the word does. RINGS for endless, because a
     ring is the one shape with no end to point at, and the sticker on the shelf is a loop for
     the same reason. DASHES for the ten-page run, laid in staggered rows like ruled marks
     counted off: a thing with a fixed number of them, which is exactly what the other side is.
     The colours are borrowed rather than invented — the oxblood of the endless sticker and the
     gold of the Play one — so the button is visibly the sticker it stands in for. */
  .btn-other { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; gap: 7px; }
  .btn-other .play-nib { width: 14px; height: 14px; flex: none; }
  .btn-other .play-loop { width: 17px; height: 10px; flex: none; }

  /* endless: rings, one bullseye to a tile */
  :is(#bonusOtherBtn.is-endless, [data-flip-id="bonusOtherBtn"].is-endless) {
    --btn-wash: rgba(168, 68, 60, 0.26);
    --btn-mark: rgba(134, 46, 40, 0.34);
    background-color: var(--btn-wash);
    background-image: repeating-radial-gradient(circle at 50% 50%,
      transparent 0 2.4px, var(--btn-mark) 2.5px 3.3px, transparent 3.4px 6.1px);
    background-size: 13px 13px;
    transition: --btn-wash 0.22s ease, --btn-mark 0.22s ease, color 0.2s ease, transform 0.12s, box-shadow 0.2s;
  }
  :is(#bonusOtherBtn.is-endless, [data-flip-id="bonusOtherBtn"].is-endless):hover {
    color: var(--paper);
    --btn-wash: #8f3a33;
    --btn-mark: rgba(245, 239, 225, 0.30);
  }

  /* the ten-page run: dashes in staggered rows, counted off */
  :is(#bonusOtherBtn.is-ten, [data-flip-id="bonusOtherBtn"].is-ten) {
    --btn-wash: rgba(199, 154, 82, 0.30);
    --btn-mark: rgba(146, 105, 38, 0.34);
    background-color: var(--btn-wash);
    background-image:
      radial-gradient(ellipse 4px 0.85px at 50% 50%, var(--btn-mark) 99%, transparent 100%),
      radial-gradient(ellipse 4px 0.85px at 50% 50%, var(--btn-mark) 99%, transparent 100%);
    background-size: 13px 7px;
    background-position: 0 0, 6.5px 3.5px;
    transition: --btn-wash 0.22s ease, --btn-mark 0.22s ease, color 0.2s ease, transform 0.12s, box-shadow 0.2s;
  }
  :is(#bonusOtherBtn.is-ten, [data-flip-id="bonusOtherBtn"].is-ten):hover {
    color: var(--paper);
    --btn-wash: #9a7526;
    --btn-mark: rgba(245, 239, 225, 0.32);
  }
  /* The row is hand-laid, so the three are not in perfect parallel. The first already leans one
     way; this one leans the other and less far, which is what stops the pair reading as a
     deliberate mirror. */
  .bg-end-actions .btn-primary:last-child { transform: rotate(-0.4deg); }

  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .bg-word, .bg-word-plain { font-size: calc(23px + var(--text-size-adjust)); }
    .bg-lyric { font-size: calc(23px + var(--text-size-adjust)); }
    .bg-sheet--ask .bg-sheet-title { font-size: calc(27px + var(--text-size-adjust)); }
    /* A redacted verse is several lines rather than one, and a block can't hyphenate, so it
       comes down further than the single-line games to keep the words off each other. */
    .bg-tok { font-size: calc(21px + var(--text-size-adjust)); }
    .bg-rline { gap: 1px 6px; }
    /* One column of full lines, so it only needs the type to come down and the marks to come
       in off a margin a phone hasn't got. */
    /* Two rows of three still fit a phone, but the scraps have to give up their padding and
       the words a couple of points. Three columns is the shape of the hand and must not become
       two: a 3+3 hand you read in one glance is the whole reason it is six. */
    .bg-hand { gap: 9px 6px; }
    .bg-scrap-paper { height: 50px; }
    .bg-who-row { gap: 6px; }
    .bg-who { min-height: 62px; }
    .bg-who-stamp { padding: 10px 6px; }
    .bg-who-word { font-size: calc(12.5px + var(--text-size-adjust)); letter-spacing: 0.1em; }
    .bg-scrap-line { font-size: calc(17px + var(--text-size-adjust)); }
    .bg-chain-text { font-size: calc(17px + var(--text-size-adjust)); }
    .bg-chain-row.is-miss .bg-chain-text { font-size: calc(13.5px + var(--text-size-adjust)); }
    .bg-chain-card { padding: 7px 9px; }
    .bg-chain-mark { left: -15px; }
    .bg-sheet-title { font-size: calc(22px + var(--text-size-adjust)); }
    .bg-back { padding: 18px 14px 13px; }
    .bg-back-head { gap: 10px; }
    .bg-back-cover { width: 40px; }
    /* The kicker goes: at this width it wraps into the stamp and pushes the game's own name
       onto two lines, and the name is the thing that has to read. */
    .bg-back-kicker { display: none; }
    .bg-back-name { font-size: calc(21px + var(--text-size-adjust)); }
    .bg-back-score { font-size: calc(34px + var(--text-size-adjust)); }
    /* The copy mark comes in a size, and the small print gives back the padding it was
       holding: on a phone the card is narrow enough that the two together wrap the footer
       onto a second line. */
    .bg-back-copy { width: 26px; height: 24px; }
    .bg-back-copy .keepsake-ic { width: 15px; height: 15px; }
    .bg-back-foot { padding: 0 26px; min-height: 24px; }
    .bg-tracks { columns: 1; }
    /* A ticked track needs no note, so the title gets the whole line. A missed one keeps it:
       that note is the answer you didn't have. */
    /* The note goes on a narrow card to buy the titles room, but not Running Order's: the time
       a page took IS that run's record, and a cleared page is the one you most want it on. It
       is short enough to keep. */
    .bg-track.ok .bg-track-note { display: none; }
    .bg-track.ok .bg-track-note.is-time { display: block; }
    /* Stacked on a phone, and now a button taller than they used to be. The corner links float
       over the bottom of the screen, so without somewhere to scroll to the last action sits
       underneath them at the very end of the page and cannot be pressed. The padding is the
       links' own height plus a thumb's worth of clearance. */
    /* Tighter on a phone, where four charms across the card's width would wrap to a word a
       line. The row still never gets a scroller: it is a handful of objects, not a list. */
    .bg-charms { margin-top: 13px; }
    .bg-charms-row { gap: 4px 13px; }
    .bg-charm-ic { width: 23px; height: 23px; }
    .bg-end-actions { flex-direction: column; gap: 9px; padding-bottom: 104px; }
    .tbt-end .bg-end-actions { padding-bottom: 0; }
    /* Stacked, so the hand-laid lean of the row's ends would read as two crooked buttons in a
       column rather than as three things laid down beside each other. */
    .bg-end-actions .btn-primary:first-child,
    .bg-end-actions .btn-primary:last-child { transform: none; }
  }
  [data-reduce-motion="on"] .bg-feedback.show,
  [data-reduce-motion="on"] .bg-reject.show { animation: none; opacity: 1; }

  /* Challenge results panel (sandboxed — in place of the records board) */
  .chall-result-status { font-family: var(--hand); font-weight: 700; font-size: calc(22px + var(--text-size-adjust)); color: var(--ink-soft); margin-bottom: 4px; }
  /* ---------- the verdict ----------
     A beaten challenge is stamped, not written. The old win line was handwriting at the same
     size as the meta under it, and it lost the argument with every other line on the screen —
     you could finish a run and not know you had won it. This is the same rubber stamp the
     drawer prints on a beaten card (red double rule, gold star), scaled up to the width of
     the panel and pressed across the top of it, so the verdict is the first thing the eye
     lands on and there is nothing to read to find out how it went.
     Multiply, because a stamp is ink sinking into paper rather than a badge sitting on it. */
  .chall-verdict { margin-bottom: 10px; }
  .chall-verdict-stamp {
    display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px;
    font-family: var(--type); font-weight: 700;
    font-size: calc(16px + var(--text-size-adjust)); letter-spacing: 3px; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: rgba(178,58,58,0.9); border: 3px double rgba(178,58,58,0.65); border-radius: 3px;
    padding: 6px 14px; transform: rotate(-2.2deg); transform-origin: 22% 60%;
    mix-blend-mode: multiply;
  }
  .chall-verdict-stamp .chall-star-svg { width: 20px; height: 20px; }
  .chall-verdict-stamp.is-dark { color: rgba(122,75,176,0.92); border-color: rgba(122,75,176,0.6); }
  /* pressed on, once, as the panel arrives: down at an angle, bedded in, settled. */
  .chall-verdict.is-win .chall-verdict-stamp { animation: verdict-press 0.5s cubic-bezier(.2,1.3,.4,1) both; }
  @keyframes verdict-press {
    0%   { opacity: 0; transform: rotate(-9deg) scale(1.45); }
    55%  { opacity: 1; transform: rotate(-1.2deg) scale(0.95); }
    100% { opacity: 1; transform: rotate(-2.2deg) scale(1); }
  }
  /* The bar the run was measured against, on a win as well as a loss: a score with no scale
     beside it is not a result. Typewriter, because it is a reading off the rules and not
     something anyone said. */
  .chall-verdict-goal {
    font-family: var(--type); font-size: calc(12px + var(--text-size-adjust));
    line-height: 1.4; color: var(--ink-soft); margin-top: 6px;
  }
  .chall-verdict-goal b { font-weight: 700; color: var(--ink); }
  .chall-verdict:not(.is-win) .chall-result-status { margin-bottom: 0; }
  :root[data-theme="dark"] .chall-verdict-stamp {
    mix-blend-mode: normal; color: #e58a84; border-color: rgba(223,107,100,0.6);
  }
  :root[data-theme="dark"] .chall-verdict-stamp.is-dark { color: rgba(208,176,255,0.95); border-color: rgba(196,154,240,0.55); }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .chall-verdict.is-win .chall-verdict-stamp { animation: none; }
  }
  [data-reduce-motion="on"] .chall-verdict.is-win .chall-verdict-stamp { animation: none; }
  /* On a phone the panel is ~250px across and the two words wrap, which leaves the star
     floating beside a two-line block. Tighten the press rather than let it break. */
  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .chall-verdict-stamp {
      font-size: calc(13px + var(--text-size-adjust)); letter-spacing: 1.8px; gap: 6px; padding: 5px 10px;
    }
    .chall-verdict-stamp .chall-star-svg { width: 16px; height: 16px; }
  }
  .chall-result-token { font-family: var(--type); font-size: calc(13px + var(--text-size-adjust)); color: #2b2722; margin-bottom: 4px; }
  .chall-result-meta { font-family: var(--type); font-size: calc(11px + var(--text-size-adjust)); color: var(--ink-soft); margin-bottom: 12px; }
  /* Two half-width buttons in a row (back to the list · replay this challenge), sitting above
     the full-width way home (the postmark, .postmark). Each of the two gets its own
     coloured-pencil wash + hand-drawn pattern so they read as distinct choices, not one repeated
     stamp: ← challenges is inked in denim blue with a dotted-notebook grid, replay is a fresh
     green with hand-ruled diagonal lines. */
  .chall-result-actions { display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-top: 8px; }
  .chall-result-actions .btn-primary { flex: 1; width: auto; margin: 0; padding: 11px 10px; font-size: calc(13px + var(--text-size-adjust)); }
  .chall-result-actions .btn-primary:first-child { transform: rotate(0.6deg); }

  /* "play the dark side" — the offer that only exists once the card above it has been
     beaten. It is deliberately NOT a third stamp in the two-up row: those two are
     stationery washed in coloured pencil, and this is the one object on a challenge results
     page that isn't made of paper at all. It sits between that row and the front-page button,
     the last thing read before leaving. A strip of night torn out of the sheet, inked in
     the dark side's aubergine with the violet bleeding into it, carrying the eclipse
     at full size with its corona drawn out. Hover and the light comes back around the blot:
     the rays extend, the paper goes darker, the hand-drawn arrow leans out of the strip. */
  .dark-invite {
    position: relative; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 13px;
    width: 100%; margin: 12px 0 0; padding: 11px 14px 11px 12px;
    text-align: left; cursor: pointer;
    color: #d7c7ee; background-color: #241a33;
    background-image:
      radial-gradient(78% 190% at 11% 52%, rgba(163,118,218,0.40) 0%, rgba(74,44,116,0.20) 46%, rgba(0,0,0,0) 78%),
      linear-gradient(168deg, rgba(255,255,255,0.10) 0%, rgba(255,255,255,0) 42%, rgba(0,0,0,0.44) 100%);
    border: 2px solid #150e1f; border-radius: 2px;
    box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px rgba(190,150,240,0.26), 3px 3px 0 rgba(26,15,42,0.34);
    transform: rotate(-0.45deg);
    transition: background-color 0.26s ease, box-shadow 0.2s ease, transform 0.12s ease;
  }

  .dark-invite-mark { flex: 0 0 auto; display: block; line-height: 0; }
  .dark-invite-svg { display: block; width: 34px; height: 34px; color: #d9c3f6; }
  /* The corona sits mostly withdrawn and comes out on hover — never a glow, just more of the
     drawing. transform-box keeps the rotation on the SVG's own centre, not the button's. */
  .dark-invite-rays {
    opacity: 0.52; transform-box: fill-box; transform-origin: center;
    transition: opacity 0.3s ease, transform 0.45s ease;
  }
  .dark-invite-text { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 2px; min-width: 0; }
  .dark-invite-title {
    font-family: var(--hand); font-weight: 700; line-height: 1;
    font-size: calc(21px + var(--text-size-adjust)); color: #f0e6fb;
  }
  .dark-invite-note {
    font-family: var(--type); font-size: calc(10.5px + var(--text-size-adjust));
    letter-spacing: 0.4px; color: #a591c4;
  }
  .dark-invite-arrow { flex: 0 0 auto; margin-left: auto; line-height: 0; color: #b79ade; }
  .dark-invite-arrow svg {
    display: block; width: 26px; height: 14px;
    transition: transform 0.22s ease;
  }

  .dark-invite:hover, .dark-invite:focus-visible {
    background-color: #1b1327;
    box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px rgba(203,166,246,0.44), 4px 4px 0 rgba(26,15,42,0.44);
  }
  .dark-invite:hover .dark-invite-rays, .dark-invite:focus-visible .dark-invite-rays {
    opacity: 0.92; transform: rotate(-9deg);
  }
  .dark-invite:hover .dark-invite-arrow svg, .dark-invite:focus-visible .dark-invite-arrow svg {
    transform: translateX(4px);
  }
  .dark-invite:active { transform: rotate(-0.45deg) translateY(1px); box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px rgba(203,166,246,0.44), 1px 1px 0 rgba(26,15,42,0.34); }

  /* Freshly unlocked: the strip arrives rather than being already there, and the corona
     swings open once before settling back. One pass, no loop. */
  .dark-invite.is-fresh { animation: darkInviteArrive 0.6s ease-out both; }
  .dark-invite.is-fresh .dark-invite-rays { animation: darkInviteCorona 2s ease-out 0.4s both; }
  @keyframes darkInviteArrive {
    from { opacity: 0; transform: rotate(-2.6deg) translateY(6px); }
    60%  { opacity: 1; transform: rotate(0.5deg) translateY(0); }
    to   { opacity: 1; transform: rotate(-0.45deg) translateY(0); }
  }
  @keyframes darkInviteCorona {
    0%   { opacity: 0; transform: rotate(-14deg); }
    35%  { opacity: 0.95; transform: rotate(0deg); }
    to   { opacity: 0.52; transform: rotate(0deg); }
  }
  [data-reduce-motion="on"] .dark-invite.is-fresh,
  [data-reduce-motion="on"] .dark-invite.is-fresh .dark-invite-rays { animation: none; }
  [data-reduce-motion="on"] .dark-invite,
  [data-reduce-motion="on"] .dark-invite-rays,
  [data-reduce-motion="on"] .dark-invite-arrow svg { transition: none; }

  /* On the night page the strip's own aubergine is nearly the paper it's taped to, and the
     hard shadow that separates it in daylight lands on black. It lifts a shade and takes a
     brighter violet hairline instead — the edge is what has to hold the object together here. */
  :root[data-theme="dark"] .dark-invite {
    background-color: #2c2041;
    box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px rgba(203,166,246,0.36), 3px 3px 0 rgba(0,0,0,0.46);
  }
  :root[data-theme="dark"] .dark-invite:hover,
  :root[data-theme="dark"] .dark-invite:focus-visible {
    background-color: #221838;
    box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px rgba(214,182,250,0.55), 4px 4px 0 rgba(0,0,0,0.54);
  }

  /* "play the light side" — the same strip inverted, offered at the end of a dark run. The
     dark side's offer is a piece of night taped to the page; this one is the page itself,
     torn out and washed in gold highlighter, so the two read as opposites at a glance rather
     than as one object in two colourways. It leans the other way for the same reason. The
     corona is already out here — on this side nothing is holding the light back — so hover
     turns it rather than extending it. */
  .light-invite {
    color: #4a3a22; background-color: #f8f2e4;
    background-image:
      radial-gradient(78% 190% at 11% 52%, rgba(226,196,124,0.28) 0%, rgba(216,200,158,0.14) 46%, rgba(0,0,0,0) 78%),
      linear-gradient(168deg, rgba(255,255,255,0.5) 0%, rgba(255,255,255,0) 44%, rgba(120,92,44,0.16) 100%);
    border-color: #4d3c22;
    box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px rgba(150,112,44,0.28), 3px 3px 0 rgba(74,56,26,0.28);
    transform: rotate(0.5deg);
  }
  .light-invite .dark-invite-svg { color: #9a6f1c; }
  .light-invite .dark-invite-rays { opacity: 0.86; }
  .light-invite .dark-invite-title { color: #352814; }
  .light-invite .dark-invite-note { color: #7b6440; }
  .light-invite .dark-invite-arrow { color: #8a6a2a; }

  .light-invite:hover, .light-invite:focus-visible {
    background-color: #fbf3e0;
    box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px rgba(150,112,44,0.45), 4px 4px 0 rgba(74,56,26,0.36);
  }
  .light-invite:hover .dark-invite-rays, .light-invite:focus-visible .dark-invite-rays {
    opacity: 1; transform: rotate(8deg);
  }
  .light-invite:active {
    transform: rotate(0.5deg) translateY(1px);
    box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px rgba(150,112,44,0.45), 1px 1px 0 rgba(74,56,26,0.28);
  }

  /* On the night page the cream strip is the brightest thing on the screen, which is the
     point — but it takes the glare off and drops the hard shadow, which has nothing left to
     fall on. */
  :root[data-theme="dark"] .light-invite {
    background-color: #ece2c9;
    box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px rgba(90,66,26,0.34), 3px 3px 0 rgba(0,0,0,0.46);
  }
  :root[data-theme="dark"] .light-invite:hover,
  :root[data-theme="dark"] .light-invite:focus-visible {
    background-color: #efe1bd;
    box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px rgba(90,66,26,0.5), 4px 4px 0 rgba(0,0,0,0.54);
  }

  :is(#backToChallenges, [data-flip-id="backToChallenges"]) {
    --btn-wash: rgba(120, 170, 220, 0.30);
